WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-US

00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:07.100
So let me-- I just
wanted to quickly review

00:00:07.100 --> 00:00:08.570
self-concordance,
just to make sure

00:00:08.570 --> 00:00:14.500
that the whole thing at
a high level is clear.

00:00:14.500 --> 00:00:19.750
The basic idea is to make
an analysis of convergence

00:00:19.750 --> 00:00:23.620
of Newton's method that
is, like Newton's method,

00:00:23.620 --> 00:00:27.550
independent of affine
changes of coordinates.

00:00:27.550 --> 00:00:29.380
And this is to correct
this embarrassment

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:32.500
that the implementation,
the actual algorithm

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:34.480
and the implementation,
is actually

00:00:34.480 --> 00:00:37.990
more sophisticated than--

00:00:37.990 --> 00:00:40.150
well, was more sophisticated
than the existing

00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:43.210
mathematical analyses
of convergence.

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:45.940
And what you'll
recall last time is

00:00:45.940 --> 00:00:48.430
that it all comes down to this.

00:00:48.430 --> 00:00:50.590
As you know, the analysis
of Newton's method

00:00:50.590 --> 00:00:54.013
comes down to how big
is the third derivative?

00:00:54.013 --> 00:00:55.430
Because that's
what it depends on.

00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:59.140
Third derivative is 0,
then Newton's method

00:00:59.140 --> 00:01:00.520
converges in one step.

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:02.458
If it's small,
that's what tells you

00:01:02.458 --> 00:01:04.750
it's going to work pretty
well, or something like that.

00:01:04.750 --> 00:01:08.320
It basically says, how fast
does the Hessian change

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:09.460
as you move around?

00:01:09.460 --> 00:01:10.360
That's what it is.

00:01:10.360 --> 00:01:10.930
OK.

00:01:10.930 --> 00:01:13.480
The classical
analysis just says--

00:01:13.480 --> 00:01:14.690
just uses that.

00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:17.930
It uses the most obvious
measure of the size

00:01:17.930 --> 00:01:19.010
of the third derivative.

00:01:19.010 --> 00:01:21.950
If it was on for n
equals 1, it would just

00:01:21.950 --> 00:01:25.207
be f prime prime prime
absolute value, which is like

00:01:25.207 --> 00:01:27.290
if someone walks up to you
in the street and says,

00:01:27.290 --> 00:01:29.570
give me a measure of the
size of the third derivative.

00:01:29.570 --> 00:01:31.460
That should be the
first thing you say.

00:01:31.460 --> 00:01:35.090
Minor problem with that is
that is not independent,

00:01:35.090 --> 00:01:37.610
that is not independent,
of changes of coordinates.

00:01:37.610 --> 00:01:41.390
And so if you're given the
homework exercise of figuring

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:47.120
out a way to say that the third
derivative is small that will

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:49.800
be independent of an affine
change of coordinates--

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:53.270
in other words,
when you do this--

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:55.370
it's going to lead you
to basically comparing

00:01:55.370 --> 00:01:58.670
the absolute value of the third
derivative to the 3/2 power

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:00.950
of the second
derivative, period.

00:02:00.950 --> 00:02:03.530
And you could put another
number for 2 there.

00:02:03.530 --> 00:02:06.570
You could divide it and call
it something else, whatever

00:02:06.570 --> 00:02:07.070
you like.

00:02:07.070 --> 00:02:09.440
But this is-- from
that point of view,

00:02:09.440 --> 00:02:11.370
otherwise this looks
like entirely mysterious,

00:02:11.370 --> 00:02:12.620
like where did that come from.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:15.295
But that's all it is.

00:02:15.295 --> 00:02:18.910
What happens when you
adopt this way to say

00:02:18.910 --> 00:02:26.170
that the Hessian varies smoothly
or not much is weird stuff

00:02:26.170 --> 00:02:27.470
happens.

00:02:27.470 --> 00:02:30.820
So a lot of functions that we
actually work with actually

00:02:30.820 --> 00:02:36.430
satisfy this property, this
so-called self-concordant

00:02:36.430 --> 00:02:39.210
property.

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:42.210
And I'm not going
to go into this.

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:45.960
And you can actually look
into how you would show this.

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:48.540
Not all convex functions-- in
fact, not all convex functions

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:50.820
that we are interested
in are self-concordant,

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:54.690
but a whole lot are.

00:02:54.690 --> 00:02:58.740
When you repeat the analysis,
an amazing thing comes out.

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:00.540
Basically, what comes
out is something

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:04.590
that I am unable to make
fun of, because the way

00:03:04.590 --> 00:03:08.130
I make fun of it-- typical
convergence analysis is, here's

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:10.860
my prototype convergence
analysis, if, and then

00:03:10.860 --> 00:03:14.190
a long list of things that you
could never possibly verify

00:03:14.190 --> 00:03:18.460
or know, hold then, and then
it gives a silly conclusion,

00:03:18.460 --> 00:03:21.870
which is also weak and
pathetic and doesn't really

00:03:21.870 --> 00:03:23.130
even say anything.

00:03:23.130 --> 00:03:26.730
So that's my model of what I see
a convergence analysis looking

00:03:26.730 --> 00:03:27.680
like.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:31.580
This, this I can't make fun
of, because it basically

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:36.650
starts by there's nothing
unknown in the hypothesis.

00:03:36.650 --> 00:03:38.390
It's just if it's
self-concordant.

00:03:38.390 --> 00:03:38.920
That's it.

00:03:38.920 --> 00:03:40.670
And we know for a whole
bunch of functions

00:03:40.670 --> 00:03:42.890
that we actually
care about, it is.

00:03:42.890 --> 00:03:44.960
And then there are
parameters in the analysis.

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:46.550
The analysis is very simple.

00:03:46.550 --> 00:03:48.240
It simply looks like this.

00:03:48.240 --> 00:03:52.800
It says-- oh, and by the
way, it's measured by lambda,

00:03:52.800 --> 00:03:55.400
which you'll remember is the
gradient times the Hessian

00:03:55.400 --> 00:03:57.230
inverse times the
gradient square--

00:03:57.230 --> 00:04:00.170
it's actually the square
root of that or something.

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:05.160
That is actually a
measure of the gradient

00:04:05.160 --> 00:04:08.210
which is independent of an
affine change of coordinates.

00:04:08.210 --> 00:04:09.940
So if you have this
beautiful algorithm,

00:04:09.940 --> 00:04:12.440
and then the last thing you
have in your code is something--

00:04:12.440 --> 00:04:14.450
is a convergence
thing that looks

00:04:14.450 --> 00:04:17.329
like norm of the
gradient, congratulations,

00:04:17.329 --> 00:04:21.206
it's now dependent,
weakly dependent,

00:04:21.206 --> 00:04:23.240
on changes of coordinates.

00:04:23.240 --> 00:04:24.410
OK.

00:04:24.410 --> 00:04:26.100
And it looks the same.

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:32.420
It basically says that for a
while, you are guaranteed--

00:04:32.420 --> 00:04:35.360
that there's a guaranteed
increase of-- decrease

00:04:35.360 --> 00:04:38.270
of gamma in the function value.

00:04:38.270 --> 00:04:41.570
And then once you're less
than this critical value eta

00:04:41.570 --> 00:04:43.700
and for lambda,
then in fact, you

00:04:43.700 --> 00:04:46.040
get this quadratic convergence.

00:04:46.040 --> 00:04:48.140
What's missing are
all those crazy things

00:04:48.140 --> 00:04:51.698
like m and l, which you
would not normally know.

00:04:51.698 --> 00:04:53.240
I mean, there would
be isolated cases

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:56.942
where I would know m and l,
but mostly you would not.

00:04:56.942 --> 00:04:59.150
Then you get a complexity
bound that looks like this.

00:04:59.150 --> 00:05:03.230
And what's amazing is that
everything here, you know.

00:05:03.230 --> 00:05:06.020
I mean-- oh, one minor-- we
still have the embarrassment

00:05:06.020 --> 00:05:07.370
of p* here.

00:05:07.370 --> 00:05:09.570
We will talk about that.

00:05:09.570 --> 00:05:12.770
We'll see this will
come up maybe next week

00:05:12.770 --> 00:05:14.030
in interior point methods.

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:16.977
We'll take care of that.

00:05:16.977 --> 00:05:18.560
You can still make
fun of it that way,

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:22.940
but it's fine because if I
replace p* with any lower bound

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:28.470
on p*, then I still get an upper
bound on the number of steps.

00:05:28.470 --> 00:05:29.520
Any-- yeah.

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:31.230
So that's the idea.

00:05:31.230 --> 00:05:32.730
OK.

00:05:32.730 --> 00:05:34.890
Yeah, and if you work this out--

00:05:34.890 --> 00:05:37.740
this is a sloppy analysis
that [INAUDIBLE] and I

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:42.100
did-- you get something like
375 times the difference.

00:05:42.100 --> 00:05:46.210
So in fact, if you do a
more careful analysis,

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:47.102
you can get like 11.

00:05:47.102 --> 00:05:49.560
I think that's the one that
Nesterov and Nemirovski came up

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:50.550
with.

00:05:50.550 --> 00:05:53.310
What's interesting
is that actually

00:05:53.310 --> 00:05:55.920
does give you the behavior.

00:05:55.920 --> 00:05:58.560
This looks like, I mean, maybe
there's a better lower bound

00:05:58.560 --> 00:05:59.790
that has this slope.

00:05:59.790 --> 00:06:02.040
We could work out
what that slope is.

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:03.870
But ours is just like
a straight-- almost a

00:06:03.870 --> 00:06:05.190
straight line going up.

00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:10.030
And 11-- yeah, 11 would
be-- eh, it's still--

00:06:10.030 --> 00:06:13.700
it's only off by a factor of
10 because it looks like if you

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:15.035
take--

00:06:15.035 --> 00:06:17.128
if you took c equals--

00:06:17.128 --> 00:06:17.670
I don't know.

00:06:17.670 --> 00:06:19.712
We could take a look at
this, what that slope is.

00:06:19.712 --> 00:06:22.320
But maybe it's like 2
or something like that?

00:06:22.320 --> 00:06:25.590
That would probably be a
pretty good estimate of it.

00:06:25.590 --> 00:06:30.450
OK, so-- now we'll talk--

00:06:30.450 --> 00:06:35.520
so just to finish up the
discussion of self-concordance,

00:06:35.520 --> 00:06:39.103
mostly it's used by people
to prove convergence

00:06:39.103 --> 00:06:40.770
of, for example,
interior point methods,

00:06:40.770 --> 00:06:42.750
which will be our next topic.

00:06:42.750 --> 00:06:44.850
So that's what it's
usually used for.

00:06:44.850 --> 00:06:47.662
People have observed
that formulations

00:06:47.662 --> 00:06:49.620
that involve self-concordant
functions actually

00:06:49.620 --> 00:06:51.580
do work better in practice.

00:06:51.580 --> 00:06:56.190
But I think no one would claim
anything strong about that.

00:06:56.190 --> 00:06:59.160
And for sure, if you were
implementing a method

00:06:59.160 --> 00:07:01.380
and something wasn't
self-concordant and it worked

00:07:01.380 --> 00:07:01.982
well against--

00:07:01.982 --> 00:07:04.440
carefully against a whole bunch
of test problems and things

00:07:04.440 --> 00:07:09.000
like that, no one would
worry about it one bit.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:14.550
Just to make sure that the
whole story on self-concordance

00:07:14.550 --> 00:07:15.090
is clear.

00:07:15.090 --> 00:07:15.630
OK.

00:07:15.630 --> 00:07:19.290
Now we get to something
much more important, which

00:07:19.290 --> 00:07:21.030
is actually the implementation.

00:07:21.030 --> 00:07:24.930
So you know that
basically, it comes down

00:07:24.930 --> 00:07:28.390
to forming this Hessian
here and the gradient,

00:07:28.390 --> 00:07:30.290
and then solving this
set of equations,

00:07:30.290 --> 00:07:32.560
which is linear algebra.

00:07:32.560 --> 00:07:35.448
And in fact, you could
even say if someone

00:07:35.448 --> 00:07:36.490
says, what are you doing?

00:07:36.490 --> 00:07:38.120
You say, I'm minimizing
a smooth function.

00:07:38.120 --> 00:07:38.995
How are you doing it?

00:07:38.995 --> 00:07:41.260
And you say, well,
I'm solving a sequence

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:44.380
of quadratic
minimization problems.

00:07:44.380 --> 00:07:46.810
And they go, uh, OK, and?

00:07:46.810 --> 00:07:49.930
And you go, ah,
quadratic minimization,

00:07:49.930 --> 00:07:51.700
that's linear algebra.

00:07:51.700 --> 00:07:55.540
So we've reduced minimizing
a smooth convex function

00:07:55.540 --> 00:07:58.630
to minimizing a sequence
of, I don't know,

00:07:58.630 --> 00:08:04.090
some tens or whatever of
quadratic minimizations, each

00:08:04.090 --> 00:08:06.497
of which is actually
just linear algebra.

00:08:06.497 --> 00:08:08.830
By the way, that means that
you can import all the stuff

00:08:08.830 --> 00:08:11.607
you know about linear algebra
with structure and banded

00:08:11.607 --> 00:08:13.190
and sparse and all
that kind of stuff,

00:08:13.190 --> 00:08:16.300
and it all just
slots right in here.

00:08:16.300 --> 00:08:17.785
OK.

00:08:17.785 --> 00:08:19.760
If there's no
structure of any kind,

00:08:19.760 --> 00:08:21.910
that's just going to
be like n cubed flops

00:08:21.910 --> 00:08:24.800
for an unstructured system.

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:28.120
If H were like sparse
or banded, it's

00:08:28.120 --> 00:08:31.210
actually going to be a
lot faster to solve--

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:33.659
I mean, if you know
what you're doing.

00:08:33.659 --> 00:08:36.870
Actually, there's many
mistakes people can make.

00:08:36.870 --> 00:08:41.650
One would be forming H
and having it be dense.

00:08:41.650 --> 00:08:43.480
Even if there's
0's all over it--

00:08:43.480 --> 00:08:45.020
I mean, you should check that.

00:08:45.020 --> 00:08:48.670
But even if there's 0's all
over it, it's not going to--

00:08:48.670 --> 00:08:50.140
some numerical
method is not going

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:51.670
to exploit that necessarily.

00:08:51.670 --> 00:08:52.210
OK.

00:08:52.210 --> 00:08:53.500
So, all right.

00:08:56.660 --> 00:08:57.680
Let's see.

00:08:57.680 --> 00:09:00.350
What you're going to
want to do is actually

00:09:00.350 --> 00:09:05.600
make a map in your head so you
know what kind of applications

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:09.590
involve a Hessian
that has structure.

00:09:09.590 --> 00:09:14.330
So here's a very
common one that occurs,

00:09:14.330 --> 00:09:17.480
is that a function
here is going to be--

00:09:17.480 --> 00:09:18.390
it's separable.

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:21.440
So it's a function-- these
are functions of one variable.

00:09:21.440 --> 00:09:24.350
These xi's are the components.

00:09:24.350 --> 00:09:25.680
So it's separable.

00:09:25.680 --> 00:09:29.240
So it's a separable
function plus a function

00:09:29.240 --> 00:09:32.930
of an affine function
of your variables.

00:09:32.930 --> 00:09:36.057
So this is extremely common
form that's going to come up.

00:09:36.057 --> 00:09:37.640
In fact, I guess
we're going to see it

00:09:37.640 --> 00:09:41.510
in just a couple of minutes
in some applications, right?

00:09:41.510 --> 00:09:45.170
So here, if you work out
what the Hessian of this is,

00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:48.200
the Hessian of a separable
function is diagonal.

00:09:48.200 --> 00:09:50.930
I mean, in fact, that's kind
of if and only if, right?

00:09:50.930 --> 00:09:53.870
If you Hessian is diagonal,
then it's the sum--

00:09:53.870 --> 00:09:56.180
it's a separable function.

00:09:56.180 --> 00:09:58.430
Because it basically means
cross-derivatives are 0.

00:09:58.430 --> 00:10:02.810
Like, partial xi
partial xj of f is 0.

00:10:02.810 --> 00:10:05.760
And so that's going to tell
you that it looks like this.

00:10:05.760 --> 00:10:07.020
OK.

00:10:07.020 --> 00:10:11.970
Now, the Hessian of this,
you can work it out.

00:10:11.970 --> 00:10:13.200
But it's actually this.

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:17.850
It's A transpose-- so here,
we're assuming A is wide.

00:10:17.850 --> 00:10:22.320
It is going to be A
transpose then H, which

00:10:22.320 --> 00:10:26.130
is the Hessian of this
thing, H0, and then times A.

00:10:26.130 --> 00:10:27.790
So if you look at
this long enough,

00:10:27.790 --> 00:10:32.400
you realize like, oh, my god,
that is diagonal plus low rank.

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:38.760
And so once again, once your
linear algebra structure center

00:10:38.760 --> 00:10:40.530
of your brain is
all up to speed,

00:10:40.530 --> 00:10:46.460
that should be blinking saying,
you can solve this faster.

00:10:46.460 --> 00:10:47.690
OK.

00:10:47.690 --> 00:10:49.520
And that's the right--

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:50.450
that's correct, right?

00:10:50.450 --> 00:10:53.210
So if you just ignore that
and form an n by n matrix

00:10:53.210 --> 00:10:55.160
then call a Cholesky
factorization,

00:10:55.160 --> 00:10:58.640
then each Newton step is going
to cost you order n cubed.

00:10:58.640 --> 00:11:02.570
So that's going to be
the naive implementation.

00:11:02.570 --> 00:11:04.670
But if you know that it's
diagonal plus low rank,

00:11:04.670 --> 00:11:05.570
you can exploit that.

00:11:05.570 --> 00:11:07.610
And that might look
something like this.

00:11:07.610 --> 00:11:11.370
First, you would do a
Cholesky factorization.

00:11:11.370 --> 00:11:14.660
This is a p cubed
operation here.

00:11:14.660 --> 00:11:18.680
Then you would write out
the Newton system as this.

00:11:18.680 --> 00:11:22.640
You'd actually literally
introduce a new variable, w.

00:11:22.640 --> 00:11:25.440
Then you eliminate delta x from
it, and you'd end up with this.

00:11:25.440 --> 00:11:27.830
And you'd recognize these
things as Schur complements

00:11:27.830 --> 00:11:30.080
of various things.

00:11:30.080 --> 00:11:33.270
And then you can work out
what everything happens here.

00:11:33.270 --> 00:11:35.330
I'm not going to go into
the details of this.

00:11:35.330 --> 00:11:38.390
But it turns out, actually
just forming this matrix

00:11:38.390 --> 00:11:40.250
here is the dominant factor.

00:11:40.250 --> 00:11:41.630
And that's 2p squared n.

00:11:41.630 --> 00:11:44.840
This is to be compared
to n cubed, right?

00:11:44.840 --> 00:11:51.120
So if p is like 1/100 of n,
then you just saved yourself

00:11:51.120 --> 00:11:52.530
10,000x.

00:11:52.530 --> 00:11:54.630
Everybody following this?

00:11:54.630 --> 00:11:57.630
So anyway, so this is the kind
of thing you're-- so when you

00:11:57.630 --> 00:12:01.470
see things like this-- and we
could do things like this--

00:12:01.470 --> 00:12:03.990
what would-- what
is the Hessian--

00:12:03.990 --> 00:12:06.450
what does it mean for
the Hessian to be banded?

00:12:06.450 --> 00:12:07.380
How about that?

00:12:10.240 --> 00:12:12.340
Just in words, what
does that mean?

00:12:17.380 --> 00:12:20.792
Is there any suggestion?

00:12:20.792 --> 00:12:23.730
Well, it just means
that there are no--

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:24.750
I'm going to call--

00:12:24.750 --> 00:12:28.200
when I take a function,
I go partial squared f

00:12:28.200 --> 00:12:29.868
partial xi partial xj.

00:12:29.868 --> 00:12:31.410
If that's not 0,
I'm going to call it

00:12:31.410 --> 00:12:33.645
an interaction between i and j.

00:12:33.645 --> 00:12:36.450
So what this says is
interactions are local.

00:12:36.450 --> 00:12:40.920
It says that those are 0 unless
i minus j is really small--

00:12:40.920 --> 00:12:43.120
like less than
half the bandwidth.

00:12:43.120 --> 00:12:44.850
So that's what it means.

00:12:44.850 --> 00:12:46.530
There's a lot of
applications that

00:12:46.530 --> 00:12:49.710
would be things like
control, signal processing.

00:12:49.710 --> 00:12:51.720
I could go on and on.

00:12:51.720 --> 00:12:55.320
I mean, these are entire fields
would have that structure.

00:12:55.320 --> 00:12:59.610
And by the way, if the Hessian
is banded, how fast can I do--

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:00.930
can I compute a Newton step?

00:13:04.840 --> 00:13:06.550
In n, if it's banded?

00:13:13.310 --> 00:13:18.680
It's just over n because the
factorization is going to be--

00:13:18.680 --> 00:13:21.140
well, it's going to actually
look exactly like this here.

00:13:21.140 --> 00:13:23.420
It's going to be this thing.

00:13:23.420 --> 00:13:25.100
So what that says
is, I don't know.

00:13:25.100 --> 00:13:28.190
I mean, just for fun, if I had
to solve an optimal control

00:13:28.190 --> 00:13:32.990
problem with, I don't know,
a couple thousand steps

00:13:32.990 --> 00:13:37.700
by Newton's method, someone
want to make a quick guess

00:13:37.700 --> 00:13:40.430
as to how long that might take?

00:13:40.430 --> 00:13:42.140
Well, let's say the
state dimension is 10

00:13:42.140 --> 00:13:43.850
and, I don't know,
there's five inputs.

00:13:43.850 --> 00:13:44.600
It doesn't matter.

00:13:44.600 --> 00:13:48.350
It's some optimal
control problem.

00:13:48.350 --> 00:13:52.520
Yeah, so if you
just wrote it out,

00:13:52.520 --> 00:13:54.800
it might take a second or two.

00:13:54.800 --> 00:13:56.990
But if you knew
what you were doing,

00:13:56.990 --> 00:14:01.280
it would be submillisecond.

00:14:01.280 --> 00:14:04.495
And this is actually how
a lot of things work.

00:14:04.495 --> 00:14:05.870
So this is the
key to everything.

00:14:05.870 --> 00:14:09.080
These are super
important things to know.

00:14:09.080 --> 00:14:11.420
You just have to
know these, period.

00:14:11.420 --> 00:14:13.370
So OK.

00:14:13.370 --> 00:14:14.620
Yeah?

00:14:14.620 --> 00:14:17.420
What happens when you
introduce the w there?

00:14:17.420 --> 00:14:19.730
Or what was the
motivation for that?

00:14:19.730 --> 00:14:22.370
Oh, this is simply
just doing it by--

00:14:22.370 --> 00:14:24.230
I mean, you could go
back and we can call it

00:14:24.230 --> 00:14:28.280
all sorts of things. w is
unelimination, if you like.

00:14:28.280 --> 00:14:29.840
Yeah, that's one way.

00:14:29.840 --> 00:14:32.030
Or this is actually just doing--

00:14:32.030 --> 00:14:35.825
this is exploiting diagonal
plus low rank or whatever.

00:14:35.825 --> 00:14:37.670
Once you expand it,
that's what happens.

00:14:37.670 --> 00:14:39.060
Yeah.

00:14:39.060 --> 00:14:40.660
So that's all this is.

00:14:40.660 --> 00:14:41.160
OK.

00:14:45.440 --> 00:14:45.950
OK.

00:14:45.950 --> 00:14:48.970
So next up-- and
this is material

00:14:48.970 --> 00:14:51.360
that you will need to
do this homework thing.

00:14:51.360 --> 00:14:52.985
Actually, we're
probably not going to--

00:14:52.985 --> 00:14:55.340
I'll say a few things
about it, but you actually

00:14:55.340 --> 00:14:58.160
are definitely going to have
to read the book carefully.

00:14:58.160 --> 00:15:02.730
It's just a couple of pages to
get the details of this method.

00:15:02.730 --> 00:15:03.230
OK.

00:15:03.230 --> 00:15:06.860
So let's look at equality
constrained minimization.

00:15:06.860 --> 00:15:11.660
So basic problem is we have
a smooth, twice continuously

00:15:11.660 --> 00:15:13.520
differentiable
convex function here.

00:15:13.520 --> 00:15:18.485
And we're going to minimize
here f subject to Ax equals b.

00:15:18.485 --> 00:15:21.410
And this basically says
these optimality conditions

00:15:21.410 --> 00:15:23.780
are necessary and sufficient.

00:15:23.780 --> 00:15:28.550
It says that when the gradient
is in the range of A transpose,

00:15:28.550 --> 00:15:32.690
and you're primal feasible,
then that's optimal.

00:15:32.690 --> 00:15:35.450
And that's if and only if.

00:15:35.450 --> 00:15:38.125
There's no-- there's
nothing else here.

00:15:38.125 --> 00:15:38.750
It's just that.

00:15:38.750 --> 00:15:41.090
No qualifications or
anything like that.

00:15:41.090 --> 00:15:43.620
This is assuming f is convex.

00:15:43.620 --> 00:15:44.120
OK.

00:15:47.580 --> 00:15:51.510
OK, so here's a classic example.

00:15:51.510 --> 00:15:52.620
It's quadratic.

00:15:52.620 --> 00:15:55.530
So here's an
equality constrained

00:15:55.530 --> 00:15:57.280
quadratic minimization problem.

00:15:57.280 --> 00:15:58.500
Looks like this, right?

00:15:58.500 --> 00:16:02.520
And the optimality condition
now, the Hessian is just P.

00:16:02.520 --> 00:16:08.010
The gradient is going to be Px
plus q or something like that.

00:16:08.010 --> 00:16:09.180
That's the gradient.

00:16:09.180 --> 00:16:10.740
And then the
optimality condition,

00:16:10.740 --> 00:16:12.840
you can write out this way.

00:16:12.840 --> 00:16:14.400
So that's what it is.

00:16:14.400 --> 00:16:19.380
This is the-- yeah, these are
the optimality conditions.

00:16:19.380 --> 00:16:23.760
These are literally these
two equations here, here.

00:16:23.760 --> 00:16:26.363
The difference is this
equation-- these are linear

00:16:26.363 --> 00:16:27.030
actually, right?

00:16:27.030 --> 00:16:28.470
That's a set of
linear equations.

00:16:28.470 --> 00:16:29.460
That's linear.

00:16:29.460 --> 00:16:33.700
And if f is quadratic, its
gradient is an affine function.

00:16:33.700 --> 00:16:36.910
And so the KKT conditions become
a set of linear equations.

00:16:36.910 --> 00:16:39.840
And once again, it's
just all linear algebra.

00:16:39.840 --> 00:16:42.770
So that-- which--

00:16:42.770 --> 00:16:43.310
good.

00:16:43.310 --> 00:16:44.180
OK.

00:16:44.180 --> 00:16:46.170
So when you set it up,
it looks like this.

00:16:46.170 --> 00:16:47.570
This is a very famous matrix.

00:16:47.570 --> 00:16:50.600
It's actually called
the KKT matrix.

00:16:50.600 --> 00:16:54.830
And it comes up in pretty much
every field I can imagine.

00:16:54.830 --> 00:16:57.250
It comes up in--
it's in mechanics.

00:16:57.250 --> 00:16:59.000
Certainly, it's in
electrical engineering.

00:16:59.000 --> 00:17:00.708
It's in lots and lots
and lots of fields.

00:17:03.170 --> 00:17:04.920
OK.

00:17:04.920 --> 00:17:06.949
So let's see.

00:17:06.949 --> 00:17:12.930
Oh, you can work out this thing
is going to be nonsingular.

00:17:12.930 --> 00:17:15.180
It is certainly not
positive definite.

00:17:15.180 --> 00:17:19.140
In fact, it's what people
call quasi-semi-definite.

00:17:19.140 --> 00:17:24.180
So this KKT matrix
here is invertible

00:17:24.180 --> 00:17:28.140
if and only if, roughly
speaking, there's nothing--

00:17:28.140 --> 00:17:32.370
the null space of A does not
intersect the null space of P.

00:17:32.370 --> 00:17:35.730
So if there's a
common vector that

00:17:35.730 --> 00:17:39.300
both satisfies Ax equals
0 and Px equals 0,

00:17:39.300 --> 00:17:42.900
then this thing is singular.

00:17:42.900 --> 00:17:46.530
And that's if and only if.

00:17:46.530 --> 00:17:50.640
And there's many, many,
many ways to express that.

00:17:50.640 --> 00:17:55.020
And you probably don't even have
to worry about that too much.

00:17:55.020 --> 00:17:56.530
But that's what that is.

00:17:56.530 --> 00:17:57.030
OK.

00:17:59.800 --> 00:18:03.910
So by the way, to
evaluate this, to solve

00:18:03.910 --> 00:18:07.460
that, we'll see that there's a
lot of options you could use.

00:18:07.460 --> 00:18:11.080
One is you could use an LDL
transpose factorization, which

00:18:11.080 --> 00:18:12.970
is-- that's a
Cholesky factorization

00:18:12.970 --> 00:18:14.660
but with different signs.

00:18:14.660 --> 00:18:18.400
And when you do that, that's
one perfectly good way.

00:18:18.400 --> 00:18:20.200
And there's others we
could talk-- we will

00:18:20.200 --> 00:18:22.690
be able to talk about later.

00:18:22.690 --> 00:18:24.650
OK.

00:18:24.650 --> 00:18:25.520
So the first thing--

00:18:25.520 --> 00:18:30.003
I don't know if you remember,
but basically in week two,

00:18:30.003 --> 00:18:32.670
I pointed out you could actually
eliminate equality constraints.

00:18:32.670 --> 00:18:35.150
You just don't need them.

00:18:35.150 --> 00:18:37.130
I mean, they don't
actually bring any--

00:18:37.130 --> 00:18:39.590
we can just get rid of them
arbitrarily, pull them back.

00:18:39.590 --> 00:18:41.010
It doesn't make any difference.

00:18:41.010 --> 00:18:43.100
And the way you do that
is if you have an equality

00:18:43.100 --> 00:18:46.820
constraint Ax equals b,
then you parameterize it

00:18:46.820 --> 00:18:49.820
as the image of an
affine function.

00:18:49.820 --> 00:18:53.630
And that requires you to
find a particular solution,

00:18:53.630 --> 00:18:55.430
say, x hat.

00:18:55.430 --> 00:18:58.340
And then you find a
matrix F, whose range

00:18:58.340 --> 00:19:02.840
is the null space of A. So
that's actually all-- yeah,

00:19:02.840 --> 00:19:05.000
that's actually all you need.

00:19:05.000 --> 00:19:07.110
And there's very
simple ways to do that.

00:19:07.110 --> 00:19:10.520
You could do a QR factorization
on A transpose or something.

00:19:10.520 --> 00:19:13.040
This is just linear
algebra at that point.

00:19:13.040 --> 00:19:14.810
OK.

00:19:14.810 --> 00:19:16.070
And that's the idea.

00:19:16.070 --> 00:19:18.840
Then what you do-- it says
that this is a parameter--

00:19:18.840 --> 00:19:23.390
this is a-- you'd call this a
free parameter parameterization

00:19:23.390 --> 00:19:26.120
of this set, which
is affine set, which

00:19:26.120 --> 00:19:28.700
is described by constraints.

00:19:28.700 --> 00:19:30.950
So that's how you'd
describe that.

00:19:30.950 --> 00:19:32.300
OK.

00:19:32.300 --> 00:19:35.690
Then you just solve this
problem because everything

00:19:35.690 --> 00:19:39.060
that satisfies Ax equals
b has exactly this form.

00:19:39.060 --> 00:19:42.020
And now this thing is just--
that's an unconstrained problem

00:19:42.020 --> 00:19:44.750
with some variable z.

00:19:44.750 --> 00:19:46.430
I could do all sorts of things.

00:19:46.430 --> 00:19:51.560
I could actually-- oh, once
I know, by the way, z*,

00:19:51.560 --> 00:19:58.620
I can reconstruct x* and even
nu* as well by these methods.

00:19:58.620 --> 00:20:01.020
OK, so that's an
equivalent problem.

00:20:01.020 --> 00:20:04.260
And this just works
by Newton's method.

00:20:04.260 --> 00:20:08.880
So we just use Newton's
method to solve that.

00:20:08.880 --> 00:20:11.930
And I'll just give
you a quick example.

00:20:11.930 --> 00:20:14.930
Here's a famous problem.

00:20:14.930 --> 00:20:16.700
It's resource allocation.

00:20:16.700 --> 00:20:21.890
So x1 up to xn are a bunch of
resources that I am going to--

00:20:21.890 --> 00:20:24.800
those are the resources
allocated to agents 1

00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:25.520
through n.

00:20:25.520 --> 00:20:28.003
That's what the xi's are.
b is my total budget.

00:20:28.003 --> 00:20:29.420
That's how much
of my resource I'm

00:20:29.420 --> 00:20:30.920
going to allocate across years.

00:20:30.920 --> 00:20:32.780
You can make the
x's non-negative.

00:20:32.780 --> 00:20:34.640
It wouldn't make any difference.

00:20:34.640 --> 00:20:37.580
And then I have a cost
function for each of my agents,

00:20:37.580 --> 00:20:43.550
like f1 of x1 is the cost
if I allocate x1 to agent 1.

00:20:43.550 --> 00:20:44.730
So that's how that works.

00:20:44.730 --> 00:20:46.640
So this is the total cost.

00:20:46.640 --> 00:20:50.150
And you have this
equality constraint.

00:20:50.150 --> 00:20:51.680
One way to eliminate it--

00:20:51.680 --> 00:20:52.670
I mean, this is easy--

00:20:52.670 --> 00:20:57.200
is what you do is you leave
x1 through xn minus 1 alone.

00:20:57.200 --> 00:20:58.310
They're going to be--

00:20:58.310 --> 00:20:59.480
we just keep them.

00:20:59.480 --> 00:21:02.630
But we write xn as b minus them.

00:21:02.630 --> 00:21:04.520
We just remove this.

00:21:04.520 --> 00:21:06.720
Then you put a function
that looks like this.

00:21:06.720 --> 00:21:10.740
So here, my z's are really
just x1 to x n minus 1.

00:21:10.740 --> 00:21:13.500
When I multiply by f--

00:21:13.500 --> 00:21:16.680
I mean, you can check that this
one-- this thing is a basis.

00:21:16.680 --> 00:21:19.050
I mean, by construction
this works.

00:21:19.050 --> 00:21:22.530
You can check that the
range of this matrix

00:21:22.530 --> 00:21:24.480
is exactly the null
space of this matrix.

00:21:24.480 --> 00:21:27.540
That matrix is just a
1's vector transpose.

00:21:27.540 --> 00:21:30.100
So that's what that
is in Ax equals b.

00:21:30.100 --> 00:21:30.600
OK.

00:21:30.600 --> 00:21:32.640
So then you end up with
this reduced problem.

00:21:32.640 --> 00:21:33.280
There you go.

00:21:33.280 --> 00:21:40.180
Oh, any comments
about that function?

00:21:40.180 --> 00:21:42.150
Anything you like about it?

00:21:42.150 --> 00:21:44.470
Can we all visualize
its Hessian, please?

00:21:44.470 --> 00:21:46.480
What is the Hessian
of this function?

00:21:51.760 --> 00:21:52.660
What is it?

00:21:52.660 --> 00:21:53.260
It's what?

00:21:53.260 --> 00:21:54.010
An arrow.

00:21:54.010 --> 00:21:56.260
You know, it's not arrow.

00:21:56.260 --> 00:21:57.550
It's very close.

00:21:57.550 --> 00:22:00.010
If you were to do one
elimination, it would be arrow.

00:22:00.010 --> 00:22:01.395
But it's very close to arrow.

00:22:01.395 --> 00:22:02.020
It's not arrow.

00:22:02.020 --> 00:22:02.603
This is dense.

00:22:02.603 --> 00:22:03.610
It's completely dense.

00:22:03.610 --> 00:22:05.620
But what is it?

00:22:05.620 --> 00:22:07.330
What's the Hessian of this part?

00:22:07.330 --> 00:22:08.190
Diagonal.

00:22:08.190 --> 00:22:08.760
Diagonal.

00:22:08.760 --> 00:22:09.360
OK.

00:22:09.360 --> 00:22:11.780
How about the Hessian of this?

00:22:11.780 --> 00:22:13.490
What is it?

00:22:13.490 --> 00:22:15.770
It's rank 1, right?

00:22:15.770 --> 00:22:16.850
You can just check.

00:22:16.850 --> 00:22:18.470
It's going to be--

00:22:18.470 --> 00:22:20.840
actually, it's literally
the matrix of 1's.

00:22:20.840 --> 00:22:24.500
It's actually 1's 1's
transpose times a number.

00:22:24.500 --> 00:22:25.760
That's what it is right.

00:22:25.760 --> 00:22:28.820
So yeah, I realize you're
just seeing this all now.

00:22:28.820 --> 00:22:30.650
But this is the
important thing--

00:22:30.650 --> 00:22:34.220
this is what you need
to do, is to be able--

00:22:34.220 --> 00:22:37.153
to actually be able to recognize
these things in the wild

00:22:37.153 --> 00:22:38.570
because you're
going to want to be

00:22:38.570 --> 00:22:39.778
able to see things like this.

00:22:39.778 --> 00:22:41.870
So yeah, so this
diagonal plus rank 1.

00:22:41.870 --> 00:22:45.290
So when you see this, you
should be able to look at that

00:22:45.290 --> 00:22:49.610
and say to someone, I can
solve that in linear time.

00:22:49.610 --> 00:22:52.040
And they would say,
wow, how do you do that?

00:22:52.040 --> 00:22:56.160
I thought Newton-- I'm using
SGD because it's too big,

00:22:56.160 --> 00:22:57.410
or I don't-- blah, blah, blah.

00:22:57.410 --> 00:22:59.120
And you're like, no, no.

00:22:59.120 --> 00:23:00.800
They go, but I have
a million variables.

00:23:00.800 --> 00:23:02.270
And you're like, too bad.

00:23:02.270 --> 00:23:04.400
I can do it on my phone.

00:23:04.400 --> 00:23:06.440
Everyone following this?

00:23:06.440 --> 00:23:09.770
So these are extremely
good things to know about.

00:23:09.770 --> 00:23:12.150
Because, I don't know,
every day there's

00:23:12.150 --> 00:23:15.300
people who don't know these
things making the wrong--

00:23:15.300 --> 00:23:18.540
arriving somewhere and
saying, oh, I tried NumPy

00:23:18.540 --> 00:23:20.010
and it was too
slow or something.

00:23:20.010 --> 00:23:20.580
Yes?

00:23:20.580 --> 00:23:23.320
I'm sorry, what is the
point of the F matrix?

00:23:23.320 --> 00:23:26.610
Because I don't see it
in the reduced problem.

00:23:26.610 --> 00:23:28.140
Oh, it is here.

00:23:28.140 --> 00:23:32.910
What I've written here, this is
actually-- this is literally--

00:23:32.910 --> 00:23:39.030
it's the original f of then
capital F z plus x hat.

00:23:39.030 --> 00:23:42.240
So I'm just writing it out
in its more natural form.

00:23:42.240 --> 00:23:44.280
But that's exactly what it is.

00:23:44.280 --> 00:23:46.500
In fact, you can sort
of see that, right?

00:23:46.500 --> 00:23:48.300
Here with this one.

00:23:48.300 --> 00:23:52.320
The F has an I stacked
on top of a minus 1's.

00:23:52.320 --> 00:23:56.070
Here's the minus 1's, and
here's the I. That's an n by 1

00:23:56.070 --> 00:23:58.560
by n minus 1 I--

00:23:58.560 --> 00:24:00.450
n minus 1 by n minus 1, yeah.

00:24:00.450 --> 00:24:01.230
OK.

00:24:01.230 --> 00:24:02.580
OK, cool?

00:24:02.580 --> 00:24:04.790
This is-- OK.

00:24:07.890 --> 00:24:11.160
So the Newton step--

00:24:11.160 --> 00:24:14.340
you can also directly get--

00:24:14.340 --> 00:24:16.710
you can actually directly
get the Newton step

00:24:16.710 --> 00:24:20.280
from the KKT system not
by doing this reduction.

00:24:20.280 --> 00:24:23.105
And so to do that,
you form this system.

00:24:23.105 --> 00:24:25.230
And this is just literally--
this is the definition

00:24:25.230 --> 00:24:26.070
of the Newton step.

00:24:26.070 --> 00:24:29.020
And it's lots of cool
interpretations of it.

00:24:29.020 --> 00:24:30.070
One is this.

00:24:30.070 --> 00:24:32.970
You'd say, well, I want to
minimize this smooth function

00:24:32.970 --> 00:24:34.740
subject to Ax equals b.

00:24:34.740 --> 00:24:38.880
And someone says, cool,
please take your function f

00:24:38.880 --> 00:24:41.700
and make a second-order
Taylor expansion, which

00:24:41.700 --> 00:24:43.830
is going to be a constant
plus a linear term

00:24:43.830 --> 00:24:46.380
plus a quadratic,
convex quadratic.

00:24:46.380 --> 00:24:49.500
And then you say you want to
minimize that subject to Ax

00:24:49.500 --> 00:24:50.220
equals b.

00:24:50.220 --> 00:24:52.380
But that matches something
you have a solver for.

00:24:52.380 --> 00:24:54.630
It's called linear-- it's
called-- you just form a KKT

00:24:54.630 --> 00:24:55.950
system and solve it.

00:24:55.950 --> 00:24:58.320
So that's one interpretation
of what this is.

00:25:00.860 --> 00:25:04.130
Another interpretation
is pretty cool, is

00:25:04.130 --> 00:25:06.200
that the so-called--
this Newton step

00:25:06.200 --> 00:25:09.350
here actually comes
from linearizing

00:25:09.350 --> 00:25:10.460
the optimality condition.

00:25:10.460 --> 00:25:14.360
So one of the optimality
conditions is already linear.

00:25:14.360 --> 00:25:17.810
This is already-- so no
linearization is needed here.

00:25:17.810 --> 00:25:20.870
It's already just a set
of linear equations.

00:25:20.870 --> 00:25:24.440
This one is going to be--

00:25:24.440 --> 00:25:27.680
this one is going to
have to be approximated.

00:25:27.680 --> 00:25:32.390
So we'll approximate this
by its second-order-- sorry,

00:25:32.390 --> 00:25:34.903
its first-order expansion
of the gradient, which

00:25:34.903 --> 00:25:37.070
is, of course, related to
the second-order expansion

00:25:37.070 --> 00:25:37.790
of the function.

00:25:37.790 --> 00:25:39.560
And we get something like this.

00:25:39.560 --> 00:25:43.310
So you can say that the Newton
step are something like this.

00:25:43.310 --> 00:25:52.790
It's a delta x
and a w for which,

00:25:52.790 --> 00:25:55.940
if you were to take that step
and if your original function

00:25:55.940 --> 00:25:57.920
was quadratic, that
would actually just

00:25:57.920 --> 00:25:59.560
be the optimal solution.

00:25:59.560 --> 00:26:00.990
That would just be the solution.

00:26:00.990 --> 00:26:02.820
Everybody got this?

00:26:02.820 --> 00:26:07.080
So this is the Newton step.

00:26:07.080 --> 00:26:09.190
OK.

00:26:09.190 --> 00:26:11.050
The Newton decrement--
we saw this

00:26:11.050 --> 00:26:13.510
before in the
unconstrained case, right?

00:26:13.510 --> 00:26:16.250
And it turns out you can write
it lots of different ways.

00:26:16.250 --> 00:26:23.710
But it is the norm of the
Newton step in the metric given

00:26:23.710 --> 00:26:24.730
by the Hessian.

00:26:24.730 --> 00:26:26.995
But maybe a better way is this.

00:26:26.995 --> 00:26:31.000
It basically says it's
the 1/2 lambda squared

00:26:31.000 --> 00:26:34.630
is actually the
predicted decrease in f

00:26:34.630 --> 00:26:37.840
if you took the Newton step and
if the function were convex--

00:26:37.840 --> 00:26:40.870
sorry, quadratic, so
that it was exact.

00:26:40.870 --> 00:26:43.490
So it's actually, as
a practical matter,

00:26:43.490 --> 00:26:49.420
a very good approximation of
how far from suboptimal you are.

00:26:49.420 --> 00:26:49.930
OK.

00:26:49.930 --> 00:26:53.170
So this is the Newton decrement.

00:26:53.170 --> 00:26:54.710
And now we get Newton's method.

00:26:54.710 --> 00:26:56.590
So here it is.

00:26:56.590 --> 00:26:59.200
You start with something
that's feasible.

00:26:59.200 --> 00:27:01.960
So Ax equals b and
x is in the domain.

00:27:01.960 --> 00:27:05.500
And you compute the
Newton step and decrement.

00:27:05.500 --> 00:27:06.422
You stop.

00:27:06.422 --> 00:27:08.380
Otherwise, you do a line
search by backtracking

00:27:08.380 --> 00:27:09.610
and then you update.

00:27:09.610 --> 00:27:12.640
Actually, you might ask,
how is this algorithm

00:27:12.640 --> 00:27:17.260
different from Newton's method
without equality constraints?

00:27:17.260 --> 00:27:18.160
Reasonable question.

00:27:18.160 --> 00:27:19.360
The answer is, it's not.

00:27:19.360 --> 00:27:20.570
They're identical.

00:27:20.570 --> 00:27:21.970
It's just the same.

00:27:21.970 --> 00:27:25.870
What's different is, for
example, when you compute

00:27:25.870 --> 00:27:29.260
the Newton step, if you
have equality constraints,

00:27:29.260 --> 00:27:32.290
you're actually solving that
KKT system, not just with

00:27:32.290 --> 00:27:32.890
the Hessian.

00:27:32.890 --> 00:27:35.320
But it's just exactly the same.

00:27:35.320 --> 00:27:37.810
So that's the idea.

00:27:37.810 --> 00:27:40.310
And this is the so-called
feasible descent method.

00:27:40.310 --> 00:27:42.280
What happens is xk--

00:27:42.280 --> 00:27:44.080
of course, x0 0 is
feasible because you

00:27:44.080 --> 00:27:46.540
have to start it with something
that satisfies Ax equals

00:27:46.540 --> 00:27:47.950
b and it's in the domain.

00:27:47.950 --> 00:27:52.750
You're always in the domain,
always have Ax equals b.

00:27:52.750 --> 00:27:56.350
And in fact, not only that, but
the function value goes down.

00:27:56.350 --> 00:27:58.550
And this algorithm
is affine invariant.

00:27:58.550 --> 00:28:01.810
So if you change coordinates
or rescale the variables,

00:28:01.810 --> 00:28:03.310
at least to first--

00:28:03.310 --> 00:28:05.110
it doesn't make any
difference at all.

00:28:05.110 --> 00:28:08.620
You get exactly the same number
of iterates-- same iterates.

00:28:08.620 --> 00:28:09.310
OK.

00:28:09.310 --> 00:28:11.830
So that's Newton's method.

00:28:11.830 --> 00:28:14.020
What's cool is that we
don't need any more--

00:28:14.020 --> 00:28:15.890
we don't need no
more analysis of it.

00:28:15.890 --> 00:28:17.500
And the reason is this.

00:28:17.500 --> 00:28:21.977
It turns out if you apply
Newton's method to this

00:28:21.977 --> 00:28:24.310
eliminate-- where you eliminate
the equality constraints

00:28:24.310 --> 00:28:26.590
like this, if you
work out what it

00:28:26.590 --> 00:28:30.160
is, it turns out it is
identical to just running

00:28:30.160 --> 00:28:32.660
Newton's method.

00:28:32.660 --> 00:28:38.930
So it's just-- it's actually
just exactly the same thing.

00:28:38.930 --> 00:28:41.090
Everything is the
same, like absolute--

00:28:41.090 --> 00:28:43.220
the iterates are identical,
the primal iterates,

00:28:43.220 --> 00:28:44.240
the dual iterates.

00:28:44.240 --> 00:28:46.770
The stopping-- the
lambda is the same.

00:28:46.770 --> 00:28:50.600
So it basically-- that
means we can actually

00:28:50.600 --> 00:28:54.470
pull in the proof of convergence
from whatever last lecture.

00:28:54.470 --> 00:28:57.440
So that's all we need.

00:28:57.440 --> 00:28:57.950
OK.

00:28:57.950 --> 00:28:58.908
So we don't ever have--

00:28:58.908 --> 00:29:02.360
we're not going to talk about
does it converge and all that?

00:29:02.360 --> 00:29:04.340
The answer is yeah, it does.

00:29:04.340 --> 00:29:06.150
And we'll see-- then
you'd say, well,

00:29:06.150 --> 00:29:08.330
what would be the difference
between these two?

00:29:08.330 --> 00:29:12.590
And this gets you
into the usual thing.

00:29:12.590 --> 00:29:14.800
In this case you say,
well, I'm eliminating.

00:29:14.800 --> 00:29:15.740
And so I'm solving--

00:29:15.740 --> 00:29:19.160
let's say you have 1,000
variables and 100 constraints.

00:29:19.160 --> 00:29:21.800
Then someone would say,
what are you doing?

00:29:21.800 --> 00:29:24.080
You'd say, well, I'm solving--

00:29:24.080 --> 00:29:26.060
I've eliminated the
equality constraints,

00:29:26.060 --> 00:29:28.280
so I'm solving a problem
with 900 variables.

00:29:28.280 --> 00:29:30.100
Everybody following this?

00:29:30.100 --> 00:29:31.600
You'd say, well,
what are you doing?

00:29:31.600 --> 00:29:32.650
And you go, yeah?

00:29:32.650 --> 00:29:35.620
I'm solving a problem
with 1,100 variables.

00:29:35.620 --> 00:29:38.410
Everybody got this?

00:29:38.410 --> 00:29:40.570
So that's if you
solve the KKT system

00:29:40.570 --> 00:29:45.820
because it's a 1,000 by 1,000
block stacked on top of a 100

00:29:45.820 --> 00:29:48.730
by 1,000 A matrix.

00:29:48.730 --> 00:29:52.000
So someone else
would look at them

00:29:52.000 --> 00:29:55.120
and say, but wait a minute,
last time I checked,

00:29:55.120 --> 00:29:58.420
1,100 cubed is bigger
than 900 cubed.

00:29:58.420 --> 00:30:00.940
And you'd say, it
is, you're right.

00:30:00.940 --> 00:30:05.050
But what we'll find is actually
that it's almost always better

00:30:05.050 --> 00:30:07.510
to solve the bigger system.

00:30:07.510 --> 00:30:08.740
So this is the other thing.

00:30:08.740 --> 00:30:11.050
If someone comes up to
you-- if the matrices have

00:30:11.050 --> 00:30:13.090
no structure at
all, obviously you'd

00:30:13.090 --> 00:30:16.760
prefer to solve a smaller
system of equations.

00:30:16.760 --> 00:30:20.800
But when there's structure,
this is, generally speaking,

00:30:20.800 --> 00:30:23.260
completely false.

00:30:23.260 --> 00:30:25.030
And it's not intuitive.

00:30:27.900 --> 00:30:29.190
OK.

00:30:29.190 --> 00:30:29.690
Aha.

00:30:29.690 --> 00:30:31.773
So now we're getting to
something which is related

00:30:31.773 --> 00:30:35.150
to something you'll
do in your homework,

00:30:35.150 --> 00:30:39.470
is the so-called
infeasible Newton--

00:30:39.470 --> 00:30:41.420
infeasible start Newton method.

00:30:41.420 --> 00:30:46.400
And what it does is it's
actually a simple extension.

00:30:46.400 --> 00:30:50.100
Before, we assumed
that x was feasible,

00:30:50.100 --> 00:30:53.360
and so Ax minus b was 0.

00:30:53.360 --> 00:30:56.180
Then this just says, well,
suppose you're not 0.

00:30:56.180 --> 00:30:58.070
What is the Newton step?

00:30:58.070 --> 00:31:02.000
And it's defined by this here.

00:31:02.000 --> 00:31:04.520
And you can write out all
sorts of cool things about it.

00:31:04.520 --> 00:31:10.610
It's linearizing the-- if
you write the residual as--

00:31:10.610 --> 00:31:12.770
well, two things here.

00:31:12.770 --> 00:31:14.810
We can have the--

00:31:14.810 --> 00:31:16.790
if you write out
this thing, if you

00:31:16.790 --> 00:31:20.030
have Ax equals-- if Ax
equals b, and this residual

00:31:20.030 --> 00:31:22.310
should be about 0,
you get these things.

00:31:22.310 --> 00:31:25.340
And the solution of this is
called the infeasible Newton

00:31:25.340 --> 00:31:26.960
step.

00:31:26.960 --> 00:31:31.250
And so this is
basically 90%, 95%

00:31:31.250 --> 00:31:33.680
of what people actually use.

00:31:33.680 --> 00:31:36.050
Or actually, since you've
been solving lots of problems,

00:31:36.050 --> 00:31:38.420
it's what [? EKOS ?] and
all sorts of other things

00:31:38.420 --> 00:31:41.090
have been using on your behalf.

00:31:41.090 --> 00:31:45.380
You didn't know it, but
that's what it was doing.

00:31:45.380 --> 00:31:48.980
You'll know all the details by
maybe next lecture or something

00:31:48.980 --> 00:31:49.670
like that.

00:31:49.670 --> 00:31:53.120
OK, so this is the infeasible
start Newton method.

00:31:53.120 --> 00:31:55.290
And there's some very
interesting things about it.

00:31:55.290 --> 00:32:00.460
First, if you take a step
of size 1, you will--

00:32:00.460 --> 00:32:02.870
then the constraints will--
then the equality constraints

00:32:02.870 --> 00:32:03.560
will hold.

00:32:03.560 --> 00:32:06.260
You will be feasible if
you take a step of 1.

00:32:06.260 --> 00:32:09.440
And you'll always be
feasible thereafter.

00:32:09.440 --> 00:32:14.450
Because once you take a step
of 1, and once x is feasible,

00:32:14.450 --> 00:32:15.650
this is 0.

00:32:15.650 --> 00:32:19.280
And this just-- it
actually becomes,

00:32:19.280 --> 00:32:21.770
except for the line search
and the stopping criterion,

00:32:21.770 --> 00:32:24.470
it's basically identical
to just Newton's method

00:32:24.470 --> 00:32:25.830
with equality constraints.

00:32:25.830 --> 00:32:28.750
That's why it's called
infeasible start.

00:32:28.750 --> 00:32:30.815
We'll see why you
might want to use this.

00:32:30.815 --> 00:32:33.190
There are some very good
reasons I'll get to in a minute.

00:32:33.190 --> 00:32:34.660
Yeah?

00:32:34.660 --> 00:32:36.520
So you said that once
it takes a step of 1,

00:32:36.520 --> 00:32:38.890
it becomes the [INAUDIBLE]
except for the stopping

00:32:38.890 --> 00:32:40.900
criterion and stuff like that.

00:32:40.900 --> 00:32:42.467
Would do in practice
take a step of 1

00:32:42.467 --> 00:32:44.050
and then change back
to the original--

00:32:44.050 --> 00:32:44.905
No.

00:32:44.905 --> 00:32:47.200
You may not be allowed
to take a step of 1.

00:32:47.200 --> 00:32:49.090
And it's a good--
so actually, what

00:32:49.090 --> 00:32:51.910
I'm about to say is highly
relevant for the homework

00:32:51.910 --> 00:32:54.130
you're going to do in
the next couple of days.

00:32:54.130 --> 00:32:55.630
You may not be
allowed-- you may not

00:32:55.630 --> 00:32:57.790
be able to take-- because,
for example, a step of 1

00:32:57.790 --> 00:33:02.285
might take you outside
the domain of f.

00:33:02.285 --> 00:33:04.430
Then so you may not--
that's one reason.

00:33:04.430 --> 00:33:08.090
We'll look at the line search
for the infeasible start Newton

00:33:08.090 --> 00:33:10.670
method actually works with--

00:33:10.670 --> 00:33:12.740
instead of working with
the function value,

00:33:12.740 --> 00:33:15.860
it actually works with
the norm of the residual.

00:33:15.860 --> 00:33:19.440
So it actually works with
the norm of the residual.

00:33:19.440 --> 00:33:20.810
So you look at--

00:33:20.810 --> 00:33:24.410
I'll call it z, right?

00:33:24.410 --> 00:33:26.120
You would look at this.

00:33:26.120 --> 00:33:31.280
z is going to be like
x stacked on top of nu.

00:33:31.280 --> 00:33:34.370
So in fact, people call z
the primal dual variable

00:33:34.370 --> 00:33:37.520
because it's basically, top
of it is the primal variable

00:33:37.520 --> 00:33:40.400
and the bottom is
the dual variable.

00:33:40.400 --> 00:33:43.100
So OK.

00:33:43.100 --> 00:33:45.650
And I think-- let's see.

00:33:45.650 --> 00:33:47.630
So here's the infeasible
start Newton method.

00:33:47.630 --> 00:33:49.550
You will implement this,
so I would strongly

00:33:49.550 --> 00:33:51.325
recommend listening.

00:33:51.325 --> 00:33:52.700
You also want to
look at the book

00:33:52.700 --> 00:33:55.830
and stuff like that to read
a couple of pages about this.

00:33:55.830 --> 00:34:00.800
So here, the initial point
does not have to satisfy Ax

00:34:00.800 --> 00:34:02.800
equals b, period.

00:34:02.800 --> 00:34:04.095
It does not have to.

00:34:04.095 --> 00:34:07.800
It must be in the
domain of your function.

00:34:07.800 --> 00:34:09.630
So if your function
was something

00:34:09.630 --> 00:34:13.883
like minus the sum of the
logs of the components,

00:34:13.883 --> 00:34:16.050
someone want to suggest a
reasonable starting point?

00:34:21.460 --> 00:34:24.219
If I say, give me
a function, give me

00:34:24.219 --> 00:34:27.820
a number that's in the
domain of negative log x,

00:34:27.820 --> 00:34:30.050
just what's the first one
that comes to your head?

00:34:30.050 --> 00:34:30.550
1.

00:34:30.550 --> 00:34:31.389
There you go, 1.

00:34:31.389 --> 00:34:32.230
Good, I like it.

00:34:32.230 --> 00:34:32.739
OK.

00:34:32.739 --> 00:34:34.690
The truth is you could have
said any positive number,

00:34:34.690 --> 00:34:36.482
and I couldn't have
said anything about it.

00:34:36.482 --> 00:34:38.889
So OK.

00:34:38.889 --> 00:34:40.790
OK, so that's the
initialization.

00:34:40.790 --> 00:34:43.449
So you compute the primal
and dual Newton steps.

00:34:43.449 --> 00:34:45.610
The backtracking
search is actually

00:34:45.610 --> 00:34:48.670
on the norm of the residual.

00:34:48.670 --> 00:34:50.560
And we have to look
at this equation

00:34:50.560 --> 00:34:52.989
pretty-- we have to look
at this sort of pseudocode

00:34:52.989 --> 00:34:54.880
pretty carefully here.

00:34:54.880 --> 00:34:57.400
This says that if
this norm does not

00:34:57.400 --> 00:35:00.730
go down by some
amount like that,

00:35:00.730 --> 00:35:04.880
then you have to
multiply t by beta.

00:35:04.880 --> 00:35:05.980
Beta is this number.

00:35:05.980 --> 00:35:08.260
Like, typically it's a 1/2
or something like that.

00:35:08.260 --> 00:35:09.968
Actually, people use
all sorts of things.

00:35:09.968 --> 00:35:11.890
But they would never--
you could use 1/2, 1/4.

00:35:11.890 --> 00:35:13.120
Some people use 0.1.

00:35:13.120 --> 00:35:15.070
It doesn't matter, it turns out.

00:35:15.070 --> 00:35:16.210
OK.

00:35:16.210 --> 00:35:20.230
Very important thing here
is one of the requirements

00:35:20.230 --> 00:35:26.390
is that x plus tx Newton has
to be in the domain of f.

00:35:26.390 --> 00:35:31.090
So let's suppose the domain
of f was r plus plus to the n.

00:35:31.090 --> 00:35:34.300
That's what it's going to be
for your homework problem.

00:35:34.300 --> 00:35:37.810
You calculate a Newton step.

00:35:37.810 --> 00:35:40.020
Here's what you do.

00:35:40.020 --> 00:35:43.120
You don't just take
x plus t delta x

00:35:43.120 --> 00:35:46.390
and then evaluate the
gradient and Hessian.

00:35:46.390 --> 00:35:49.970
Because if x is
outside the domain--

00:35:49.970 --> 00:35:53.080
so an entry of x is
negative, for example, or 0,

00:35:53.080 --> 00:35:54.850
then the gradient--

00:35:54.850 --> 00:35:57.410
actually, you're really
going to be messed up,

00:35:57.410 --> 00:36:00.160
especially if you had formulas
like the gradient is 1 over x--

00:36:00.160 --> 00:36:01.345
minus 1 over x--

00:36:01.345 --> 00:36:03.100
and the Hessian is
1 over x squared.

00:36:03.100 --> 00:36:07.870
Unfortunately, those things will
evaluate correctly to a number

00:36:07.870 --> 00:36:09.850
even if x is negative.

00:36:09.850 --> 00:36:11.080
Everybody following this?

00:36:11.080 --> 00:36:13.840
But nothing good is going
to happen after that.

00:36:13.840 --> 00:36:16.930
Absolutely nothing good
is going to happen.

00:36:16.930 --> 00:36:18.580
Your algorithm will not work.

00:36:18.580 --> 00:36:20.630
It's complete-- nothing
will happen, right?

00:36:20.630 --> 00:36:25.030
So that's why I almost wish
that they had in math libraries

00:36:25.030 --> 00:36:28.250
you could simply say the
domain of-- actually,

00:36:28.250 --> 00:36:31.160
no, you'll evaluate-- if you
were to evaluate-- so these are

00:36:31.160 --> 00:36:33.950
interesting because you actually
don't evaluate f anywhere here.

00:36:33.950 --> 00:36:36.590
But if you did, you'd know you
were in trouble because you'd

00:36:36.590 --> 00:36:39.051
get an error saying,
hey, you're trying

00:36:39.051 --> 00:36:43.100
to evaluate the log of
a negative number or 0.

00:36:43.100 --> 00:36:45.802
And that's not going to
go very well unless you

00:36:45.802 --> 00:36:48.010
have a complex library or
something like that, right?

00:36:48.010 --> 00:36:48.790
OK.

00:36:48.790 --> 00:36:50.950
So the way to do
this, then-- so what

00:36:50.950 --> 00:36:52.840
that means is if this
is not in the domain,

00:36:52.840 --> 00:36:55.700
this isn't even defined, this
just means it's infinity.

00:36:55.700 --> 00:36:58.720
So you would take a t, check--

00:36:58.720 --> 00:37:02.800
check if x plus t
delta x is positive.

00:37:02.800 --> 00:37:07.930
If it is not positive,
don't evaluate this.

00:37:07.930 --> 00:37:10.330
Just times t times equals beta.

00:37:10.330 --> 00:37:13.630
And you keep doing that
until you're actually

00:37:13.630 --> 00:37:15.650
in the domain, which
means it's positive,

00:37:15.650 --> 00:37:17.403
then you can evaluate this.

00:37:17.403 --> 00:37:18.820
So I don't know
if that was clear,

00:37:18.820 --> 00:37:22.060
but maybe to those working
on this already, it is.

00:37:22.060 --> 00:37:24.430
So OK.

00:37:24.430 --> 00:37:27.220
So I think that was
enough of a hint.

00:37:27.220 --> 00:37:29.200
But it's an important one.

00:37:29.200 --> 00:37:32.020
So OK.

00:37:32.020 --> 00:37:34.000
So this is it.

00:37:34.000 --> 00:37:39.370
And the basic idea of this is
that the directional derivative

00:37:39.370 --> 00:37:43.130
is, in fact, also equal to
minus the norm of the residual.

00:37:43.130 --> 00:37:46.430
So what that says is if
you're doing a line search,

00:37:46.430 --> 00:37:47.600
it's pretty cool.

00:37:47.600 --> 00:37:48.500
It looks like this.

00:37:48.500 --> 00:37:50.495
This is t.

00:37:50.495 --> 00:37:51.350
This is the norm--

00:37:51.350 --> 00:37:54.950
I'm going to plot here
the norm of the residual.

00:37:54.950 --> 00:37:57.620
So at t equals 0, it's
at your current point.

00:37:57.620 --> 00:38:00.170
And what it says is that
the slope of this curve

00:38:00.170 --> 00:38:02.480
is exactly minus 1.

00:38:02.480 --> 00:38:07.550
So if you're going to make that
step backtracking or Armijo

00:38:07.550 --> 00:38:10.160
step or whatever you call it,
it's going to look like this.

00:38:10.160 --> 00:38:13.040
What it really-- it's just
going to look like that.

00:38:13.040 --> 00:38:16.130
And so you will
degrade this by alpha.

00:38:16.130 --> 00:38:19.130
And you will take the first
time you get something

00:38:19.130 --> 00:38:20.180
that is below that curve.

00:38:20.180 --> 00:38:22.730
And you absolutely have to
because if t is small enough,

00:38:22.730 --> 00:38:23.840
it's got to happen.

00:38:23.840 --> 00:38:27.140
So OK, yeah.

00:38:27.140 --> 00:38:30.800
You actually did this, even
though yours isn't quite--

00:38:30.800 --> 00:38:33.800
there's some questions as to
whether or not it's working.

00:38:33.800 --> 00:38:35.790
I think I made this
error, probably.

00:38:35.790 --> 00:38:36.290
OK.

00:38:36.290 --> 00:38:37.220
Well, sorry.

00:38:37.220 --> 00:38:38.800
That's what you
get for doing it--

00:38:38.800 --> 00:38:39.890
so we should have done--

00:38:39.890 --> 00:38:41.600
yeah, anyway, that's a timing.

00:38:41.600 --> 00:38:42.380
That's right.

00:38:42.380 --> 00:38:43.590
That's a good experience.

00:38:43.590 --> 00:38:45.530
It's a good life
experience to have.

00:38:45.530 --> 00:38:49.520
Everybody should have--
you should actually at once

00:38:49.520 --> 00:38:51.228
in their life write
