00:38:51.228 --> 00:38:53.270
or something like that
where they were not-- they

00:38:53.270 --> 00:38:57.260
were too cavalier about domains,
and nothing-- and it was bad.

00:38:57.260 --> 00:38:58.550
So OK.

00:38:58.550 --> 00:38:59.930
All right.

00:38:59.930 --> 00:39:04.880
So you'll be implementing
this for the homework.

00:39:04.880 --> 00:39:06.650
OK.

00:39:06.650 --> 00:39:10.910
Let's talk about how you
solve the KKT system.

00:39:10.910 --> 00:39:14.510
Well, one obvious thing, which
is, by the way, not bad at all,

00:39:14.510 --> 00:39:17.930
is actually just doing LDL
transpose factorization.

00:39:17.930 --> 00:39:21.530
So here's good news about
LDL transpose factorization,

00:39:21.530 --> 00:39:25.670
is if you use a good
sparse package, then--

00:39:25.670 --> 00:39:28.910
I mean, I'm assuming
H and A are sparse.

00:39:28.910 --> 00:39:31.370
Then it's actually going
to exploit all the--

00:39:31.370 --> 00:39:34.310
it's going to exploit a
lot of structure there,

00:39:34.310 --> 00:39:36.110
more even than you know about.

00:39:36.110 --> 00:39:39.530
So I mean, you could do
this for a control problem,

00:39:39.530 --> 00:39:43.430
signal processing-- all sorts
of stuff you could just do this.

00:39:43.430 --> 00:39:46.460
You can do elimination.

00:39:46.460 --> 00:39:47.780
This is often done.

00:39:47.780 --> 00:39:52.430
If H is diagonal, so if the
Hessian is diagonal-- like,

00:39:52.430 --> 00:39:56.030
for example, if the function
is separable, it's separable

00:39:56.030 --> 00:39:59.150
but they're coupled by
the equality constraints,

00:39:59.150 --> 00:40:00.890
then H will be diagonal.

00:40:00.890 --> 00:40:05.270
And in that point,
that's tempting enough

00:40:05.270 --> 00:40:07.340
for you to do elimination on it.

00:40:07.340 --> 00:40:09.650
So you would eliminate
because we know the inverse.

00:40:09.650 --> 00:40:11.810
H could be banded-- same story.

00:40:11.810 --> 00:40:15.350
So I mean, here's an
example of a banded one.

00:40:15.350 --> 00:40:20.180
Optimal control with
a constraint that--

00:40:20.180 --> 00:40:23.173
some constraints that
couple states far apart.

00:40:23.173 --> 00:40:24.590
For example, you're
making a tour.

00:40:24.590 --> 00:40:26.840
You say, make me a
little drone tour.

00:40:26.840 --> 00:40:29.910
And it's got to start and
end at the same place.

00:40:29.910 --> 00:40:31.080
Well, OK.

00:40:31.080 --> 00:40:34.590
So when I hear that, when
I hear optimal control,

00:40:34.590 --> 00:40:37.440
I visualize immediately
a banded system.

00:40:37.440 --> 00:40:39.090
I know immediately
the banded system

00:40:39.090 --> 00:40:41.730
is going to drop down somewhere
deep in the linear algebra.

00:40:41.730 --> 00:40:43.950
I'm going to solve with banded
system, can do that super fast.

00:40:43.950 --> 00:40:45.540
But when I say
something like, you

00:40:45.540 --> 00:40:48.390
have to be back at
the same place, that

00:40:48.390 --> 00:40:50.400
actually couples the
beginning position

00:40:50.400 --> 00:40:51.383
and the final position.

00:40:51.383 --> 00:40:52.800
And that's actually
one of these--

00:40:52.800 --> 00:40:54.717
that's going to be an
equality constraint that

00:40:54.717 --> 00:40:58.380
breaks the bandedness.

00:40:58.380 --> 00:41:01.930
But I'll also note that
I can do that super fast.

00:41:01.930 --> 00:41:04.650
So in other words,
that's just a nonproblem.

00:41:04.650 --> 00:41:05.710
OK.

00:41:05.710 --> 00:41:06.210
All right.

00:41:06.210 --> 00:41:11.370
So to eliminate here H
inverse-- and usually,

00:41:11.370 --> 00:41:15.090
this is done when H is
something like diagonal.

00:41:15.090 --> 00:41:18.870
So you would just do a
block elimination here.

00:41:18.870 --> 00:41:23.280
And you'd end up with
something looking like that.

00:41:23.280 --> 00:41:28.080
So you would first-- you
would form this matrix, then

00:41:28.080 --> 00:41:31.970
factorize it, then solve.

00:41:31.970 --> 00:41:34.263
And H inverse, you
shouldn't even worry about,

00:41:34.263 --> 00:41:35.180
because it's diagonal.

00:41:35.180 --> 00:41:37.700
So it's kind of a silly thing.

00:41:37.700 --> 00:41:40.910
And then you'd end up
with something like that.

00:41:40.910 --> 00:41:43.310
So this is-- once you form--

00:41:43.310 --> 00:41:46.100
this would allow
you to solve for w.

00:41:46.100 --> 00:41:49.310
Once you solve for w, you
solve for v over here, right?

00:41:49.310 --> 00:41:50.360
And that's cheap.

00:41:50.360 --> 00:41:51.290
Well, it's silly.

00:41:51.290 --> 00:41:54.210
H is diagonal, so that
would be-- that's the idea.

00:41:54.210 --> 00:41:54.710
OK.

00:41:57.400 --> 00:41:59.320
All right.

00:41:59.320 --> 00:42:02.590
OK, so we're going to look at--

00:42:02.590 --> 00:42:04.710
so actually, what's weird
about all these things

00:42:04.710 --> 00:42:06.753
is that they're all
kind of the same,

00:42:06.753 --> 00:42:09.420
all these different-- so now you
have lots of different methods,

00:42:09.420 --> 00:42:10.170
right?

00:42:10.170 --> 00:42:13.020
By the way, this is shockingly
close to what you will

00:42:13.020 --> 00:42:15.550
be solving in your homework.

00:42:15.550 --> 00:42:17.550
So again, you might
want to listen up

00:42:17.550 --> 00:42:19.260
as to how all this works.

00:42:19.260 --> 00:42:24.370
So we now have all
sorts of different ways.

00:42:24.370 --> 00:42:29.130
So you could say, yeah,
I could solve that.

00:42:29.130 --> 00:42:30.270
It's smooth problem.

00:42:30.270 --> 00:42:32.550
And you can say, oh, I
could solve the dual here.

00:42:32.550 --> 00:42:34.860
And in this case,
you could actually--

00:42:34.860 --> 00:42:38.100
you could reconstruct the
solution of the primal

00:42:38.100 --> 00:42:38.850
from the dual.

00:42:38.850 --> 00:42:42.390
It's not always true, but it
is if the objective is strictly

00:42:42.390 --> 00:42:43.980
convex, which it is here.

00:42:43.980 --> 00:42:47.140
So you could say--
you could do this.

00:42:47.140 --> 00:42:47.640
All right.

00:42:47.640 --> 00:42:50.220
So let's just do
the simple thing.

00:42:50.220 --> 00:42:51.720
Actually, here's
what's interesting.

00:42:51.720 --> 00:42:55.350
They all have different
initialization requirements.

00:42:55.350 --> 00:42:57.780
And actually, that's
actually what's

00:42:57.780 --> 00:42:59.130
going to actually be the--

00:42:59.130 --> 00:43:01.588
you're going to see all these
methods are going to turn out

00:43:01.588 --> 00:43:03.140
to be essentially the same.

00:43:03.140 --> 00:43:04.090
They're not the same.

00:43:04.090 --> 00:43:07.420
But in spirit, the work you
do per iteration, if you know

00:43:07.420 --> 00:43:08.900
what you're doing, is the same.

00:43:08.900 --> 00:43:10.983
So there's just no difference
between any of them.

00:43:10.983 --> 00:43:13.160
So-- well, except
the initialization.

00:43:13.160 --> 00:43:16.510
So if we just do Newton's method
with equality constraints, that

00:43:16.510 --> 00:43:21.260
says someone has got to come
up with an x that is positive,

00:43:21.260 --> 00:43:24.480
strictly positive, and
satisfies Ax equals b.

00:43:24.480 --> 00:43:27.880
If these are just general A's--
if this is just a general A

00:43:27.880 --> 00:43:32.152
and b, how would
you find such an x?

00:43:32.152 --> 00:43:33.860
I guess you'd have to
solve-- in general,

00:43:33.860 --> 00:43:37.060
you'd have to solve
an LP to do that.

00:43:37.060 --> 00:43:40.870
And this is bad because this
is maybe simpler than an LP.

00:43:40.870 --> 00:43:44.080
And it's a general rule
that you should not

00:43:44.080 --> 00:43:47.170
have a subroutine that's more
complicated than just solving

00:43:47.170 --> 00:43:48.400
the problem itself.

00:43:48.400 --> 00:43:54.078
So on the other hand, sometimes
it is easy to come up with a--

00:43:54.078 --> 00:43:55.870
even though there's an
equality constraint,

00:43:55.870 --> 00:43:57.280
sometimes it's easy to do.

00:43:57.280 --> 00:43:59.950
How about resource
allocation where--

00:43:59.950 --> 00:44:01.090
here's the constraint.

00:44:01.090 --> 00:44:01.660
Ready?

00:44:01.660 --> 00:44:03.580
The sum of the x's is equal--

00:44:03.580 --> 00:44:05.708
I'm going to make my budget b.

00:44:05.708 --> 00:44:06.250
There you go.

00:44:06.250 --> 00:44:07.920
Sum of the x's is equal to b.

00:44:07.920 --> 00:44:12.520
Can someone suggest-- and
suppose that all of the--

00:44:12.520 --> 00:44:13.390
well, let's see.

00:44:13.390 --> 00:44:16.630
Suppose all of the functions
that I want to minimize--

00:44:16.630 --> 00:44:19.570
or actually, you would call this
log utility, which is actually

00:44:19.570 --> 00:44:20.440
widely used.

00:44:20.440 --> 00:44:24.310
So this would be log utility
allocation of resources

00:44:24.310 --> 00:44:26.920
or something like that.

00:44:26.920 --> 00:44:29.480
Someone got a suggestion for--

00:44:29.480 --> 00:44:31.730
can you think of
a positive vector

00:44:31.730 --> 00:44:34.130
that-- so b is a scalar
for us now, right?

00:44:34.130 --> 00:44:35.555
It's sum xi equals b.

00:44:35.555 --> 00:44:36.830
That's the budget.

00:44:36.830 --> 00:44:40.580
So I'm deciding how much I/O
bandwidth or something like

00:44:40.580 --> 00:44:43.040
that a bunch of processes get.

00:44:43.040 --> 00:44:46.190
So somebody-- how
would you-- what's

00:44:46.190 --> 00:44:47.570
an initial resource allocation?

00:44:47.570 --> 00:44:48.080
What?

00:44:48.080 --> 00:44:49.430
b divided by n.

00:44:49.430 --> 00:44:49.940
Perfect.

00:44:49.940 --> 00:44:50.540
Uniform.

00:44:50.540 --> 00:44:53.720
Yeah, you just do
each xi is b over n,

00:44:53.720 --> 00:44:56.660
and you put a comment in the
code that says we initialize

00:44:56.660 --> 00:44:59.900
with uniform allocation.

00:44:59.900 --> 00:45:00.470
Done.

00:45:00.470 --> 00:45:01.910
So then you can do this.

00:45:01.910 --> 00:45:03.060
OK.

00:45:03.060 --> 00:45:03.560
All right.

00:45:03.560 --> 00:45:05.185
So here's what happens
if you run this.

00:45:05.185 --> 00:45:07.850
I think this is just run from
several different initial

00:45:07.850 --> 00:45:08.640
conditions.

00:45:08.640 --> 00:45:10.610
And you see your
classic Newton thing.

00:45:10.610 --> 00:45:12.020
This is what you're looking for.

00:45:12.020 --> 00:45:15.300
This is a log plot,
and this is linear.

00:45:15.300 --> 00:45:16.470
This is a semi-log plot.

00:45:16.470 --> 00:45:19.310
And you're looking
for this acceleration.

00:45:19.310 --> 00:45:20.660
That's when you declare success.

00:45:20.660 --> 00:45:24.980
That's Newton's-- that's how
you know you actually have

00:45:24.980 --> 00:45:26.900
an implementation
of Newton's method.

00:45:26.900 --> 00:45:27.620
OK.

00:45:27.620 --> 00:45:29.360
So OK.

00:45:29.360 --> 00:45:30.650
And you have that.

00:45:30.650 --> 00:45:31.400
OK, that's one.

00:45:34.290 --> 00:45:38.940
We could actually apply
Newton's method to the dual.

00:45:38.940 --> 00:45:41.160
And let's actually see.

00:45:41.160 --> 00:45:42.235
I think-- let's see.

00:45:42.235 --> 00:45:44.610
Did I say what the-- oh, I
did say what the numbers were.

00:45:44.610 --> 00:45:44.880
Aha.

00:45:44.880 --> 00:45:45.380
OK.

00:45:45.380 --> 00:45:49.560
So here, I said that there's
100 equality constraints and 500

00:45:49.560 --> 00:45:50.970
variables, right?

00:45:50.970 --> 00:45:56.100
So in this problem,
yeah, this is--

00:45:56.100 --> 00:46:00.450
we have basically 500 variables.

00:46:00.450 --> 00:46:03.467
But then someone says, oh, no,
no, I learned about this dual.

00:46:03.467 --> 00:46:04.300
It's really awesome.

00:46:04.300 --> 00:46:06.150
Plus, you can-- it
sounds fancy, and so you

00:46:06.150 --> 00:46:08.850
can impress your friends if
they're gullible or whatever.

00:46:08.850 --> 00:46:11.710
And you can say, oh, no, no,
no, to solve that resource

00:46:11.710 --> 00:46:12.210
allocation--

00:46:12.210 --> 00:46:14.513
I'm going to solve the
