00:47:51.900 --> 00:47:52.770
it's not even funny.

00:47:52.770 --> 00:47:53.790
So OK.

00:47:53.790 --> 00:47:56.460
So we'll get to that.

00:47:56.460 --> 00:47:58.675
That's a fun part in the end.

00:47:58.675 --> 00:48:01.050
Then you'd say, well, there's
the infeasible start Newton

00:48:01.050 --> 00:48:01.550
method.

00:48:01.550 --> 00:48:04.920
This is the only one that
has an extremely simple

00:48:04.920 --> 00:48:06.810
initialization.

00:48:06.810 --> 00:48:09.000
You don't have to
satisfy Ax equals b.

00:48:09.000 --> 00:48:11.790
You only have to satisfy that
you're in the domain of f.

00:48:11.790 --> 00:48:14.070
f is the sum of negative logs.

00:48:14.070 --> 00:48:16.590
The domain is r
plus plus to the n.

00:48:16.590 --> 00:48:20.340
So you initialize with 1's or
your favorite other positive

00:48:20.340 --> 00:48:22.440
vector, and you're done.

00:48:22.440 --> 00:48:24.180
And you'd say, look,
here's what it is.

00:48:24.180 --> 00:48:26.430
And they'd say, oh,
you're doing 22 steps.

00:48:26.430 --> 00:48:28.320
That's-- anyway, this
is all just silly.

00:48:28.320 --> 00:48:30.540
So OK.

00:48:30.540 --> 00:48:34.080
Let's look a little bit more
closely at what happens.

00:48:34.080 --> 00:48:37.260
Oh, by the way, these methods
would be wildly different

00:48:37.260 --> 00:48:41.340
if you used naive
linear algebra.

00:48:41.340 --> 00:48:43.440
If you used naive
numerical linear algebra,

00:48:43.440 --> 00:48:46.410
they would be wild-- if you
actually formed a KKT matrix

00:48:46.410 --> 00:48:49.275
or whatever, the full
thing, and solved it,

00:48:49.275 --> 00:48:52.080
this would be different.

00:48:52.080 --> 00:48:54.270
But if you actually do the
analysis to figure out--

00:48:54.270 --> 00:48:57.660
assuming you actually know
how to use linear algebra,

00:48:57.660 --> 00:49:00.570
then it turns out,
well, it's not shocking.

00:49:00.570 --> 00:49:03.870
They're all actually
identical in complexity.

00:49:03.870 --> 00:49:05.230
We'll see how that works.

00:49:05.230 --> 00:49:10.230
So to use Newton's method, you
use block elimination on this.

00:49:10.230 --> 00:49:12.000
And you would end
up solving something

00:49:12.000 --> 00:49:15.030
that looks like A times
the diagonal matrix

00:49:15.030 --> 00:49:16.470
times A transpose.

00:49:16.470 --> 00:49:22.920
That's what you'd-- you'd form
and solve that system, OK?

00:49:22.920 --> 00:49:24.780
OK.

00:49:24.780 --> 00:49:26.280
Notice that this
is the smaller one.

00:49:26.280 --> 00:49:29.860
That's the 100 by
100 linear system.

00:49:29.860 --> 00:49:33.370
If you wanted to
do the dual, you'd

00:49:33.370 --> 00:49:35.590
actually have to work
out what is the Hessian

00:49:35.590 --> 00:49:37.840
of the dual objective.

00:49:37.840 --> 00:49:40.588
And it turns out that looks--
it's going to be identical.

00:49:40.588 --> 00:49:42.880
You're going to solve something
that looks like A times

00:49:42.880 --> 00:49:44.560
the diagonal matrix,
positive matrix,

00:49:44.560 --> 00:49:46.840
times A transpose
times delta nu.

00:49:46.840 --> 00:49:50.350
And this is just the
detail of what it is.

00:49:50.350 --> 00:49:52.630
So once again, you
would solve a 100

00:49:52.630 --> 00:49:57.730
by 100 set of linear equations.

00:49:57.730 --> 00:49:58.400
OK.

00:49:58.400 --> 00:50:00.760
If you use block elimination,
you end up with this.

00:50:00.760 --> 00:50:01.810
Oh, hey, wait a minute.

00:50:01.810 --> 00:50:03.340
That's identical to this.

00:50:03.340 --> 00:50:05.590
The only thing different is
that this is 0 because you

00:50:05.590 --> 00:50:07.900
are assuming x is feasible.

00:50:07.900 --> 00:50:09.430
And down here, we put in--

00:50:09.430 --> 00:50:13.540
we actually are putting
in the residual.

00:50:13.540 --> 00:50:15.370
So this need not be 0.

00:50:15.370 --> 00:50:16.420
If you are not--

00:50:16.420 --> 00:50:20.830
if you're not yet feasible,
then b minus Ax is not 0.

00:50:20.830 --> 00:50:22.870
And this is going
to actually reduce

00:50:22.870 --> 00:50:25.700
to solving this,
exactly the same system,

00:50:25.700 --> 00:50:28.000
but with a different
right-hand side.

00:50:28.000 --> 00:50:30.730
That's the difference.

00:50:30.730 --> 00:50:34.720
So the conclusion
is that basically,

00:50:34.720 --> 00:50:38.020
in all three of these methods
that look wildly different--

00:50:38.020 --> 00:50:40.780
and someone could
try to argue they

00:50:40.780 --> 00:50:44.140
were wildly different, right?

00:50:44.140 --> 00:50:45.370
It's all the same.

00:50:45.370 --> 00:50:48.370
By the way, this is the
same generally for duality,

00:50:48.370 --> 00:50:52.035
that if someone says, oh--

00:50:52.035 --> 00:50:54.160
you know, especially for
Newton's method and things

00:50:54.160 --> 00:50:55.060
like that, they
say, well, no, I'm

00:50:55.060 --> 00:50:57.340
going to use this to solve
the dual or something.

00:50:57.340 --> 00:51:00.190
For methods like this,
they're no different if you

00:51:00.190 --> 00:51:01.757
use smart linear algebra.

00:51:01.757 --> 00:51:04.090
If you use naive linear
algebra, they're very different.

00:51:04.090 --> 00:51:07.160
There are reasons to solve
a dual instead of a primal,

00:51:07.160 --> 00:51:08.670
but it's much more advanced.

00:51:08.670 --> 00:51:11.830
It has to do with if you want a
distributed method or something

00:51:11.830 --> 00:51:12.430
like that.

00:51:12.430 --> 00:51:14.870
Then it can start
making a difference.

00:51:14.870 --> 00:51:19.235
But for a lot of these,
there's no difference at all.

00:51:19.235 --> 00:51:19.735
OK.

00:51:22.540 --> 00:51:27.800
Let's look at-- this is actually
a real and cool example.

00:51:27.800 --> 00:51:32.000
So let me just show
you what it is.

00:51:32.000 --> 00:51:36.850
It's, I guess, flow
utility maximization.

00:51:36.850 --> 00:51:38.270
So it looks like this.

00:51:38.270 --> 00:51:39.550
I have a graph.

00:51:39.550 --> 00:51:45.160
So I have some vertices.

00:51:45.160 --> 00:51:47.005
And I can-- I'll
make it directed.

00:51:50.080 --> 00:51:51.712
So here's my graph.

00:51:51.712 --> 00:51:53.170
I mean, this doesn't
matter, right?

00:51:53.170 --> 00:51:56.680
But I'm just going to draw
a picture of it like this.

00:51:56.680 --> 00:51:58.360
There you go.

00:51:58.360 --> 00:52:00.460
OK, there's my graph.

00:52:00.460 --> 00:52:02.380
And then what I
imagine is that there's

00:52:02.380 --> 00:52:05.020
each xi is going to
represent a flow on an edge.

00:52:05.020 --> 00:52:07.660
So there's a flow here.

00:52:07.660 --> 00:52:12.160
By the way, x can be negative,
as those of you in EE

00:52:12.160 --> 00:52:12.970
would know.

00:52:12.970 --> 00:52:15.010
This is called the
reference direction.

00:52:15.010 --> 00:52:19.240
X negative says simply that the
flow of whatever it really is,

00:52:19.240 --> 00:52:23.320
like, for example,
electricity or current--

00:52:23.320 --> 00:52:24.982
charge, I should say--

00:52:24.982 --> 00:52:26.440
it means if it's
negative, it means

00:52:26.440 --> 00:52:30.550
it goes in the opposite
direction of what's

00:52:30.550 --> 00:52:32.530
called the reference direction.

00:52:32.530 --> 00:52:34.870
Everybody got this, right?

00:52:34.870 --> 00:52:38.090
And this comes up
in a lot of things.

00:52:38.090 --> 00:52:41.200
Ax equals b is actually--

00:52:41.200 --> 00:52:45.730
Ax equals 0 says that you
have flow conservation if A

00:52:45.730 --> 00:52:47.905
is the incidence matrix.

00:52:47.905 --> 00:52:49.690
That would be flow conservation.

00:52:49.690 --> 00:52:51.940
And in fact, you would call--

00:52:51.940 --> 00:52:53.650
you'd call an x
with Ax equals 0,

00:52:53.650 --> 00:52:57.310
that's called a
circulation because it's

00:52:57.310 --> 00:53:01.660
something where, at each
node, you have conservation.

00:53:01.660 --> 00:53:04.360
And so an x that
satisfies that is

00:53:04.360 --> 00:53:06.280
called a circulation
because-- here's

00:53:06.280 --> 00:53:07.450
an example of a circulation.

00:53:07.450 --> 00:53:10.720
I have a flow of 1, 1, 1, 1.

00:53:10.720 --> 00:53:13.000
It's just-- it's
a flow in a circle

00:53:13.000 --> 00:53:16.670
here, which would be-- that
would be a circulation.

00:53:16.670 --> 00:53:19.780
When you have b, what
it corresponds to is

00:53:19.780 --> 00:53:26.450
that you actually have someone
is injecting at each node b.

00:53:26.450 --> 00:53:31.200
And again, this could either be
injecting or pulling stuff out.

00:53:31.200 --> 00:53:33.080
Depends on what it is.

00:53:33.080 --> 00:53:37.493
So this is what a network
would look like for that.

00:53:37.493 --> 00:53:39.410
Here, I'll put another
edge here just for fun.

00:53:39.410 --> 00:53:41.750
So this is my network.

00:53:41.750 --> 00:53:43.880
And this would have
lots of examples.

00:53:43.880 --> 00:53:48.050
Oh, some people call it a
single commodity flow problem.

00:53:48.050 --> 00:53:50.720
"Single commodity" means it's
a single commodity, which is

00:53:50.720 --> 00:53:53.090
whatever is moving across here.

00:53:53.090 --> 00:53:55.460
It could be charge in
an electrical circuit.

00:53:55.460 --> 00:53:57.650
This could be a
power grid, where

00:53:57.650 --> 00:54:01.370
we are not taking into
account transmission losses.

00:54:01.370 --> 00:54:05.900
So if someone is pumping
power in, that's a generator.

00:54:05.900 --> 00:54:10.550
If they're pulling it
off, that is a load.

00:54:10.550 --> 00:54:14.570
And then this is the power
flows on all these transmission

00:54:14.570 --> 00:54:15.530
lines.

00:54:15.530 --> 00:54:19.550
This is neglecting losses in
the transmission line, which

00:54:19.550 --> 00:54:22.040
is actually not a bad
thing to-- it's a perfectly

00:54:22.040 --> 00:54:23.920
good model for some of this.

00:54:23.920 --> 00:54:25.240
So this is what this is.

00:54:25.240 --> 00:54:27.640
But this could be
lots of other stuff.

00:54:27.640 --> 00:54:31.560
This could be flows of
money in a banking system,

00:54:31.560 --> 00:54:35.410
could be all sorts of stuff--

00:54:35.410 --> 00:54:39.970
flow of mass or something
like that, movement of a drug

00:54:39.970 --> 00:54:42.910
from different
compartments in a person.

00:54:42.910 --> 00:54:43.960
That's another one.

00:54:43.960 --> 00:54:46.400
But OK.

00:54:46.400 --> 00:54:46.900
All right.

00:54:46.900 --> 00:54:50.390
So this is network
flow optimization.

00:54:50.390 --> 00:54:50.890
OK.

00:54:50.890 --> 00:54:55.780
So the matrix A is
super duper sparse

00:54:55.780 --> 00:55:00.850
because actually, each
column has at most--

00:55:00.850 --> 00:55:05.140
it has exactly two non-zeros.

00:55:05.140 --> 00:55:07.030
It's got a plus 1 and a minus 1.

00:55:07.030 --> 00:55:10.270
Because each column
corresponds to one edge here.

00:55:10.270 --> 00:55:12.110
And one edge goes
between two vertices,

00:55:12.110 --> 00:55:14.950
so this is exactly
two non-zeros.

00:55:14.950 --> 00:55:17.140
So A is--

00:55:17.140 --> 00:55:21.220
I guess A tilde because we're
going to reduce it in a minute.

00:55:21.220 --> 00:55:24.820
Yeah, so A tilde
is going to be--

00:55:24.820 --> 00:55:28.300
the A tilde is the so-called
node incidence matrix.

00:55:28.300 --> 00:55:30.430
That's not full rank.

00:55:30.430 --> 00:55:34.300
So you'd remove one row
and refer to-- by the way,

00:55:34.300 --> 00:55:38.050
you do that and you refer to
one of the nodes as ground.

00:55:38.050 --> 00:55:41.860
Now I'm speaking EE dialect,
but that's what you do.

00:55:41.860 --> 00:55:44.500
So OK.

00:55:44.500 --> 00:55:45.730
Let's see.

00:55:45.730 --> 00:55:50.750
How do I know that
this is not full rank?

00:55:50.750 --> 00:55:53.240
I mean, how do I know that
if I write a matrix that

00:55:53.240 --> 00:55:57.344
looks like this, here--

00:56:01.580 --> 00:56:05.340
if I make a matrix
that looks like that,

00:56:05.340 --> 00:56:09.126
how do I know it's
not full rank?

00:56:09.126 --> 00:56:12.080
[INAUDIBLE] it becomes
a reduced form.

00:56:12.080 --> 00:56:12.590
Yeah.

00:56:12.590 --> 00:56:17.240
So you-- if you just add
up all the rows, you get 0.

00:56:17.240 --> 00:56:22.430
So that means 1
transpose A is 0.

00:56:22.430 --> 00:56:25.400
And 1 is not-- the
1's vector is not 0,

00:56:25.400 --> 00:56:27.350
so that means it's
not full rank.

00:56:27.350 --> 00:56:29.190
So anyway.

00:56:29.190 --> 00:56:30.930
I won't go into the details.

00:56:30.930 --> 00:56:34.090
This is all-- this is just
very good stuff to have seen.

00:56:34.090 --> 00:56:36.215
Actually, how many people
have seen stuff like this

00:56:36.215 --> 00:56:38.780
in some class somewhere?

00:56:38.780 --> 00:56:40.490
OK, good.

00:56:40.490 --> 00:56:42.470
What context?

00:56:42.470 --> 00:56:43.460
Flux balance.

00:56:43.460 --> 00:56:45.140
Oh, flux balance in biology.

00:56:45.140 --> 00:56:46.130
OK, good.

00:56:46.130 --> 00:56:46.670
Good.

00:56:46.670 --> 00:56:47.510
Yeah.

00:56:47.510 --> 00:56:48.500
OK, you, too?

00:56:48.500 --> 00:56:49.790
Yeah.

00:56:49.790 --> 00:56:51.320
Linear programming class.

00:56:51.320 --> 00:56:52.340
Yeah, OK.

00:56:52.340 --> 00:56:56.150
Linear programming, good,
good, and flux balance.

00:56:56.150 --> 00:56:58.100
OK.

00:56:58.100 --> 00:56:59.150
All right.

00:56:59.150 --> 00:57:02.300
So let's look at the KKT system
for this specific problem.

00:57:02.300 --> 00:57:05.000
Oh, so you're looking
at this and you'd say,

00:57:05.000 --> 00:57:07.790
I don't know, how easy--

00:57:07.790 --> 00:57:11.885
OK, California grid
has about 3,100x--

00:57:11.885 --> 00:57:13.970
nah, it's about 6,000
come to think of it.

00:57:13.970 --> 00:57:16.400
Sorry, there's about
3,100 generators,

00:57:16.400 --> 00:57:19.670
and there's about maybe 6,000
transmission lines, roughly,

00:57:19.670 --> 00:57:21.050
in California.

00:57:21.050 --> 00:57:23.830
So the question would be--

00:57:23.830 --> 00:57:26.720
that's actually not-- that
problem doesn't scare me,

00:57:26.720 --> 00:57:28.220
because even if you
were really dumb

00:57:28.220 --> 00:57:29.970
and just did it with
dents and everything,

00:57:29.970 --> 00:57:33.290
it would be-- it would
still be just fine, right?

00:57:33.290 --> 00:57:34.383
But if I took like--

00:57:34.383 --> 00:57:34.925
I don't know.

00:57:34.925 --> 00:57:38.180
If I took the US
Western grid, then it

00:57:38.180 --> 00:57:40.490
would not be the case anymore.

00:57:40.490 --> 00:57:43.820
We would start getting
50,000, 100,000--

00:57:43.820 --> 00:57:47.630
we'd be getting some
big problems there.

00:57:47.630 --> 00:57:52.220
But when you see this, you
should probably-- lots of signs

00:57:52.220 --> 00:57:53.720
should be blinking.

00:57:53.720 --> 00:57:56.480
Number one, your
objective is diagonal.

00:57:56.480 --> 00:57:59.520
So-- sorry, the
Hessian of your--

00:57:59.520 --> 00:58:02.160
your objective is
separable, which

00:58:02.160 --> 00:58:03.657
means your Hessian is diagonal.

00:58:03.657 --> 00:58:05.740
So that should just-- the
first time you see this,

00:58:05.740 --> 00:58:09.210
you should just
say, OK, I'm primed.

00:58:09.210 --> 00:58:13.590
I'm ready to do some
actual linear algebra here.

00:58:13.590 --> 00:58:16.110
Then you see Ax equals b,
and you say, what is that?

00:58:16.110 --> 00:58:18.235
And you say, oh, that's
this node incidence matrix.

00:58:18.235 --> 00:58:21.450
Every column has exactly
two non-zero entries.

00:58:21.450 --> 00:58:23.340
Then your sparsity
part of your brain

00:58:23.340 --> 00:58:25.350
should be like now
blinking, right?

00:58:25.350 --> 00:58:28.620
And so at this point, you
should actually depending on,

00:58:28.620 --> 00:58:30.302
I don't know-- you
could say to whoever

00:58:30.302 --> 00:58:32.260
asked you to solve this
problem, you could say,

00:58:32.260 --> 00:58:35.010
well, we're going
to be able to solve

00:58:35.010 --> 00:58:37.200
that shockingly efficiently.

00:58:37.200 --> 00:58:40.660
And they'd say, yeah, but I
have like a million x's and 10

00:58:40.660 --> 00:58:41.250
million--

00:58:41.250 --> 00:58:44.430
I have a million nodes
in my power network,

00:58:44.430 --> 00:58:46.650
and I have 10 million edges.

00:58:46.650 --> 00:58:47.490
Really?

00:58:47.490 --> 00:58:48.883
And you go, Yeah.

00:58:48.883 --> 00:58:51.050
They'll say, are you going
to use a gradient method?

00:58:51.050 --> 00:58:54.740
And you're like, no, dude,
we're going full Newton on this.

00:58:54.740 --> 00:58:56.240
And they're like
how, could you do--

00:58:56.240 --> 00:58:57.350
you see what I'm saying, right?

00:58:57.350 --> 00:58:59.240
There are still people
stuck in this mindset.

00:58:59.240 --> 00:59:02.570
Actually, I'd say the vast
majority would not know this.

00:59:02.570 --> 00:59:04.100
So anyway.

00:59:04.100 --> 00:59:05.030
And you'd say, yeah.

00:59:05.030 --> 00:59:06.380
They'd say, well,
how can you do that?

00:59:06.380 --> 00:59:07.730
You can't even form the Hessian.

00:59:07.730 --> 00:59:08.480
And they're right.

00:59:08.480 --> 00:59:10.123
You'd say, yeah, you're right.

00:59:10.123 --> 00:59:11.540
I'm not going to
form the Hessian.

00:59:11.540 --> 00:59:13.352
I'm going to solve a KKT system.

00:59:13.352 --> 00:59:14.810
I'm going to exploit
the structure.

00:59:14.810 --> 00:59:16.102
And I'll be able to do it fast.

00:59:16.102 --> 00:59:17.985
And that's how.

00:59:17.985 --> 00:59:18.485
OK.

00:59:22.350 --> 00:59:23.580
So here it is.

00:59:23.580 --> 00:59:28.500
That's our diagonal
part of the Hessian.

00:59:28.500 --> 00:59:30.210
We'll do some elimination here.

00:59:30.210 --> 00:59:32.520
And you'll end up computing--

00:59:32.520 --> 00:59:34.170
forming this matrix here.

00:59:34.170 --> 00:59:36.510
By the way, this matrix
is, if you've heard of it,

00:59:36.510 --> 00:59:38.460
it's a Laplacian matrix.

00:59:38.460 --> 00:59:40.980
So in circuits, this
would be literally

00:59:40.980 --> 00:59:42.450
the conductance matrix.

00:59:42.450 --> 00:59:45.120
Again, that's how you
would say it in EE.

00:59:45.120 --> 00:59:47.700
But in other fields,
it's got other names.

00:59:47.700 --> 00:59:51.330
So this is a conductance
matrix or a Laplacian.

00:59:51.330 --> 00:59:54.930
"Laplacian" means the
diagonals are positive,

00:59:54.930 --> 00:59:58.650
the offdiagonals
are nonpositive,

00:59:58.650 --> 01:00:03.660
and the sum of each
row is going to be 0.

01:00:03.660 --> 01:00:06.060
That's if we don't do the
reduced one, but that's fine.

01:00:06.060 --> 01:00:08.268
Actually, if we do the
reduced one, that's fine, too.

01:00:08.268 --> 01:00:10.710
So everybody got that?

01:00:10.710 --> 01:00:15.670
So this is-- oh, by the
way, this thing here,

01:00:15.670 --> 01:00:18.990
solving so-called Laplacian
equations like this,

01:00:18.990 --> 01:00:21.180
that's like a
whole subindustry--

01:00:21.180 --> 01:00:24.330
mostly of computer--
theoretical computer science.

01:00:24.330 --> 01:00:30.120
So if you type that into Google,
you'll find lots of stuff--

01:00:30.120 --> 01:00:33.000
mostly, to be perfectly bluntly
honest about it, completely

01:00:33.000 --> 01:00:34.530
and utterly useless.

01:00:34.530 --> 01:00:37.710
There are very good methods
to do it that are not those.

01:00:37.710 --> 01:00:38.700
So OK.

01:00:41.630 --> 01:00:42.130
OK.

01:00:42.130 --> 01:00:43.030
So let's see.

01:00:43.030 --> 01:00:46.480
You can even say this thing,
this Laplacian matrix,

01:00:46.480 --> 01:00:49.060
is actually-- we know the
sparsity pattern of it

01:00:49.060 --> 01:00:51.130
immediately, which
is really cool.

01:00:51.130 --> 01:00:52.468
It's basically this.

01:00:55.780 --> 01:00:57.950
It's basically nodes by nodes.

01:00:57.950 --> 01:01:00.850
So if I had a million nodes,
this would be a million

01:01:00.850 --> 01:01:02.020
by million matrix.

01:01:02.020 --> 01:01:07.390
But what it says is
that nodes i and j,

01:01:07.390 --> 01:01:09.160
only if they're
connected by an arc

01:01:09.160 --> 01:01:11.710
would it be true that
this matrix here,

01:01:11.710 --> 01:01:15.820
the Schur complement, is
going to have a non-zero.

01:01:15.820 --> 01:01:17.590
So if I told you
my original power

01:01:17.590 --> 01:01:20.680
network had 10 million nodes--

01:01:20.680 --> 01:01:23.800
a million nodes,
10 million edges--

01:01:23.800 --> 01:01:25.870
and suppose I told
you that there weren't

01:01:25.870 --> 01:01:30.760
weird things where there's not
one thing with 50,000 edges

01:01:30.760 --> 01:01:31.990
adjacent to it.

01:01:31.990 --> 01:01:40.300
If I said, look, the degree of
a node varies between 2 and 50,

01:01:40.300 --> 01:01:42.460
Then that tells you that
the number of non-zeros

01:01:42.460 --> 01:01:46.960
in this matrix, per row,
is between 10 and 50.

01:01:46.960 --> 01:01:48.190
Everybody following this?

01:01:48.190 --> 01:01:52.360
And that's going to qualify
as a sparse matrix, OK?

01:01:52.360 --> 01:01:55.300
Everybody following this?

01:01:55.300 --> 01:01:55.930
Right?

01:01:55.930 --> 01:02:01.990
So what it means is that you can
solve network flow optimization

01:02:01.990 --> 01:02:08.630
problems for, I don't know,
very large problems if you want.

01:02:08.630 --> 01:02:11.140
And if you know how to do it.

01:02:11.140 --> 01:02:12.250
OK?

01:02:12.250 --> 01:02:13.150
So right.

01:02:13.150 --> 01:02:15.470
Did all that make sense?

01:02:15.470 --> 01:02:15.970
Yeah.

01:02:18.940 --> 01:02:19.630
OK.

01:02:19.630 --> 01:02:22.370
We'll look at something-- this
is a little bit more esoteric,

01:02:22.370 --> 01:02:25.190
but just to show
you that the ideas--

01:02:25.190 --> 01:02:28.210
I mean, you just use these
ideas whenever you can.

01:02:28.210 --> 01:02:31.160
So on any specific problem,
you work out what they are.

01:02:31.160 --> 01:02:34.990
So here's one where our variable
is actually a matrix capital

01:02:34.990 --> 01:02:37.360
X, which is--

01:02:37.360 --> 01:02:40.840
it's n by n, my matrix.

01:02:40.840 --> 01:02:46.120
So one option for this would be
to string out all the entries

01:02:46.120 --> 01:02:47.920
of X. X is symmetric.

01:02:47.920 --> 01:02:50.500
So there would be n n
plus 1 over 2 entries

01:02:50.500 --> 01:02:53.490
if I vectorized
X. Because that's

01:02:53.490 --> 01:02:58.170
the number of distinct
elements in a symmetric matrix.

01:02:58.170 --> 01:03:00.930
So then I could
compute the Hessian,

01:03:00.930 --> 01:03:07.020
which would be n n plus 1 over
2 by n n plus 1 over 2 matrix.

01:03:07.020 --> 01:03:08.600
Everybody following this?

01:03:08.600 --> 01:03:09.100
OK.

01:03:09.100 --> 01:03:13.560
So that's got n to the
fourth entries in it, right?

01:03:13.560 --> 01:03:16.470
Then if I just naively--

01:03:16.470 --> 01:03:23.790
oh, then I stack A on top of
it, I would form a KKT system.

01:03:23.790 --> 01:03:26.220
Anyway, I'd end up
with a giant matrix

01:03:26.220 --> 01:03:29.040
that was basically n to the
fourth by n to the fourth.

01:03:29.040 --> 01:03:30.240
Everybody following this?

01:03:30.240 --> 01:03:33.570
So you know,
something n equals 200

01:03:33.570 --> 01:03:35.400
is going to be a
kind of a showstopper

01:03:35.400 --> 01:03:39.900
or something like that, because
it's just going to be too big.

01:03:39.900 --> 01:03:41.650
But in theory, it would work.

01:03:41.650 --> 01:03:44.700
You could actually do this,
run this, and then reassemble X

01:03:44.700 --> 01:03:46.500
into a matrix and all that.

01:03:46.500 --> 01:03:49.733
So it turns out we'll do
a lot better than that.

01:03:49.733 --> 01:03:51.150
By the way, each
Newton step there

01:03:51.150 --> 01:03:54.620
would cost you n to the sixth.

01:03:54.620 --> 01:03:55.760
Here's why.

01:03:55.760 --> 01:03:58.820
The number of variables
is really n squared.

01:03:58.820 --> 01:04:01.040
Because I used n
to say X is n by n.

01:04:01.040 --> 01:04:03.200
It's n squared over 2 roughly.

01:04:03.200 --> 01:04:05.480
Then if I form this
matrix, my storage

01:04:05.480 --> 01:04:06.800
is going to be n to the fourth.

01:04:06.800 --> 01:04:09.082
And the computation per
iteration is going to be--

01:04:09.082 --> 01:04:11.540
what would normally be-- it's
the size of the matrix cubed.

01:04:11.540 --> 01:04:14.570
But if that's n squared, that's
going to be n to the sixth.

01:04:14.570 --> 01:04:15.770
Everybody following this?

01:04:15.770 --> 01:04:18.840
So OK, that would be bad.

01:04:18.840 --> 01:04:21.290
By the way, you might ask
who uses this kind of stuff.

01:04:21.290 --> 01:04:24.050
This comes up-- ooh, I could
give an interpretation of that

01:04:24.050 --> 01:04:26.190
in probability, in
stochastic processes.

01:04:26.190 --> 01:04:29.930
I could-- in control, things
like this come up all the time.

01:04:29.930 --> 01:04:31.290
But it comes up.

01:04:31.290 --> 01:04:33.320
So things like this,
people actually do solve.

01:04:33.320 --> 01:04:33.890
OK.

01:04:33.890 --> 01:04:37.100
Now we'll do it the right way.

01:04:37.100 --> 01:04:39.950
So the right way is to write
down the optimality conditions

01:04:39.950 --> 01:04:42.410
here for this problem.

01:04:42.410 --> 01:04:45.440
Oh, and the truth
is you don't want

01:04:45.440 --> 01:04:49.250
to calculate the Hessian
of the determinant

01:04:49.250 --> 01:04:51.920
of the inverse of a matrix.

01:04:51.920 --> 01:04:54.980
I mean, I can tell
you what it is,

01:04:54.980 --> 01:04:58.070
but you do not want
to write it out.

01:04:58.070 --> 01:05:00.590
Actually, you can.

01:05:00.590 --> 01:05:02.750
You can write it out as an
abstract linear operator

01:05:02.750 --> 01:05:03.410
pretty easily.

01:05:03.410 --> 01:05:06.150
So let's look how this works.

01:05:06.150 --> 01:05:09.382
So the conditions are that--

01:05:09.382 --> 01:05:11.090
I mean, it's got to
be positive definite.

01:05:11.090 --> 01:05:12.890
Otherwise, this makes no sense.

01:05:12.890 --> 01:05:16.280
And then the gradient of
this, I'm not too afraid of,

01:05:16.280 --> 01:05:18.590
because that's minus X inverse.

01:05:18.590 --> 01:05:20.240
So that's the gradient.

01:05:20.240 --> 01:05:23.150
The Hessian is not
too bad, either.

01:05:23.150 --> 01:05:24.950
But OK.

01:05:24.950 --> 01:05:27.810
So I write this out like this.

01:05:27.810 --> 01:05:30.450
And so these are the equations
I really want to solve.

01:05:30.450 --> 01:05:33.020
And in fact, what
the Newton method

01:05:33.020 --> 01:05:38.225
is doing is it's actually
just linearizing--

01:05:38.225 --> 01:05:39.080
these are linear.

01:05:39.080 --> 01:05:40.880
They don't need
to be linearized.

01:05:40.880 --> 01:05:43.580
These are not linear,
because of this,

01:05:43.580 --> 01:05:44.870
and you just linearize that.

01:05:44.870 --> 01:05:46.880
When you linearize this,
you end up with that.

01:05:46.880 --> 01:05:50.540
That's the derivative of
the inverse of a matrix.

01:05:50.540 --> 01:05:54.290
And it makes sense
because minus 1 over X,

01:05:54.290 --> 01:05:57.740
If you take the derivative
of that, is 1 over X squared.

01:05:57.740 --> 01:05:59.210
That's for scalars.

01:05:59.210 --> 01:06:03.472
For matrices, it's hardly--

01:06:03.472 --> 01:06:06.680
then the only question
is, how do you say X--

01:06:06.680 --> 01:06:08.717
1 over X squared in matrices?

01:06:08.717 --> 01:06:11.300
And there weren't too many ways,
but this is the way to do it.

01:06:11.300 --> 01:06:11.810
OK?

01:06:11.810 --> 01:06:13.950
So you get that.

01:06:13.950 --> 01:06:18.690
So we have to solve
these equations here.

01:06:18.690 --> 01:06:22.800
And remember, we're going to try
to find this matrix, delta X.

01:06:22.800 --> 01:06:27.930
And we're going to try to find
these dual variables, w, j.

01:06:27.930 --> 01:06:29.160
OK.

01:06:29.160 --> 01:06:30.870
So this is the idea.

01:06:30.870 --> 01:06:34.830
And you have a
bunch of variables.

01:06:34.830 --> 01:06:37.058
You have basically
n squared variables.

01:06:37.058 --> 01:06:38.850
And all we're doing
is-- like, this is just

01:06:38.850 --> 01:06:41.610
block elimination
but doing it manually

01:06:41.610 --> 01:06:45.100
for this particular problem.

01:06:45.100 --> 01:06:49.920
So what you do is you eliminate
delta X from the first equation

01:06:49.920 --> 01:06:51.330
by multiplying.

01:06:51.330 --> 01:06:52.530
You go back over here.

01:06:52.530 --> 01:06:57.720
You multiply this equation by
X on the left and the right.

01:06:57.720 --> 01:07:00.330
And you'll get like
an X and an X--

01:07:00.330 --> 01:07:01.580
X and X over here.

01:07:01.580 --> 01:07:03.920
This will become X.
And that will become--

01:07:03.920 --> 01:07:06.440
this will become
delta X over here.

01:07:06.440 --> 01:07:09.860
And you end up with
this equation here.

01:07:09.860 --> 01:07:12.110
Then you substitute that
in the second equation,

01:07:12.110 --> 01:07:15.440
and you get this system
of equations here.

01:07:15.440 --> 01:07:18.260
And that's a dense
positive definite system

01:07:18.260 --> 01:07:20.220
of linear equations,
which you then solve.

01:07:20.220 --> 01:07:23.360
But if you work out the whole
thing, what's going to happen

01:07:23.360 --> 01:07:26.570
is you have different-- you
have various things here.

01:07:26.570 --> 01:07:28.730
p is small.

01:07:28.730 --> 01:07:32.210
But the main thing
that's going to cost you

01:07:32.210 --> 01:07:36.350
is going to be the piece like p
squared n squared or p n cubed,

01:07:36.350 --> 01:07:38.510
this kind of thing.

01:07:38.510 --> 01:07:43.400
And notice that the entire
thing is now quadratic in n,

01:07:43.400 --> 01:07:49.040
whereas the super naive method
was the sixth power in n.

01:07:49.040 --> 01:07:53.030
So that's the same as
going from n cubed to n,

01:07:53.030 --> 01:07:56.330
going from cubic to linear.

01:07:56.330 --> 01:07:58.140
And that's what's
happened here, once

01:07:58.140 --> 01:08:00.600
again, except the
number of variables

01:08:00.600 --> 01:08:01.830
is like n squared over 2.

01:08:01.830 --> 01:08:02.940
So that's the same thing.

01:08:02.940 --> 01:08:06.780
It went from n to the
sixth to n squared.

01:08:06.780 --> 01:08:09.480
That's a big difference, right?

01:08:09.480 --> 01:08:10.050
OK.

01:08:10.050 --> 01:08:13.390
So and-- oh, yes,
you didn't know it,

01:08:13.390 --> 01:08:15.060
but this has been
done on your behalf.

01:08:15.060 --> 01:08:18.510
Any time you ever
solved an SDP, this

01:08:18.510 --> 01:08:21.210
is what was going
on in the solver.

01:08:21.210 --> 01:08:23.100
You don't need to
know it, but that's--

01:08:23.100 --> 01:08:24.359
which is kind of the point.

01:08:24.359 --> 01:08:24.930
OK.

01:08:24.930 --> 01:08:32.729
So this finishes up the equality
constrained minimization.

01:08:32.729 --> 01:08:36.180
You will do a homework
problem on it.

01:08:36.180 --> 01:08:40.605
Oh, I was going to
say it's worthwhile--

01:08:40.605 --> 01:08:41.920
spend some time on it.

01:08:41.920 --> 01:08:44.308
But I would hope if
you-- at some point,

01:08:44.308 --> 01:08:46.350
if you've been working on
it for two, three hours

01:08:46.350 --> 01:08:48.270
and still debugging, you
should have the sense

01:08:48.270 --> 01:08:51.538
to just step away
from the keyboard,

01:08:51.538 --> 01:08:53.580
hold your hands up where
other people can see it,

01:08:53.580 --> 01:08:55.260
and then just walk away.

01:08:55.260 --> 01:09:00.170
I just say this because I've
even had people come and say,

01:09:00.170 --> 01:09:02.810
I've been working on this for
hours, we'll look at the code

01:09:02.810 --> 01:09:06.085
together, and I can't
figure out what's wrong.

01:09:06.085 --> 01:09:10.010
So this is just a little--
this is just slightly

01:09:10.010 --> 01:09:13.412
over the threshold where you're
not writing a 10-line script.

01:09:13.412 --> 01:09:14.870
Actually, in the
end, it's probably

01:09:14.870 --> 01:09:16.370
going to be like 15 lines.

01:09:16.370 --> 01:09:20.359
But it's just entirely possible
that, I don't know, something--

01:09:20.359 --> 01:09:23.540
So I'm just saying,
by all means try.

01:09:23.540 --> 01:09:27.950
But somewhere around the
second or third hour,

01:09:27.950 --> 01:09:31.430
feel free to quit.

01:09:31.430 --> 01:09:33.770
Just because there's
no real good reason

01:09:33.770 --> 01:09:35.990
to sit there
debugging something.

01:09:35.990 --> 01:09:38.779
And what you're looking
for is you're looking

01:09:38.779 --> 01:09:40.220
for that quadratic convergence.

01:09:40.220 --> 01:09:42.560
When you see an error
curve that goes like this

01:09:42.560 --> 01:09:46.819
and then accelerates,
then you're done.

01:09:46.819 --> 01:09:47.564
Maybe.

01:09:47.564 --> 01:09:49.189
You're already seeing
that, but there's

01:09:49.189 --> 01:09:51.647
some other suspicious things
in your implementation, right?

01:09:51.647 --> 01:09:52.910
So yeah.

01:09:52.910 --> 01:09:55.070
OK, everybody cool?

01:09:55.070 --> 01:09:56.930
OK.

01:09:56.930 --> 01:10:00.330
Now we're going to go
on to the last section.

01:10:00.330 --> 01:10:02.670
So actually, we're
building a hierarchy.

01:10:02.670 --> 01:10:05.340
And we went from
the very low level.

01:10:05.340 --> 01:10:08.400
Low level is linear algebra.

01:10:08.400 --> 01:10:10.770
Interestingly,
linear algebra is how

01:10:10.770 --> 01:10:14.490
you solve quadratic
optimization-- sorry, equality

01:10:14.490 --> 01:10:17.070
constrained quadratic
minimization problems.

01:10:17.070 --> 01:10:21.330
So linear algebra, you could
just call it minimizing--

01:10:21.330 --> 01:10:25.320
it's effective
methods for solving--

01:10:25.320 --> 01:10:28.830
for minimizing quadratic--
convex quadratic subject

01:10:28.830 --> 01:10:31.330
to linear equality constraints.

01:10:31.330 --> 01:10:33.540
Then you say, well, now I
know-- now I'm showing you

01:10:33.540 --> 01:10:36.560
a method for minimizing smooth
functions subject to equality

01:10:36.560 --> 01:10:37.060
constraints.

01:10:37.060 --> 01:10:38.435
Someone says, how
do you do that?

01:10:38.435 --> 01:10:41.490
You go, I approximate the
smooth function as a quadratic

01:10:41.490 --> 01:10:43.260
in 22 steps.

01:10:43.260 --> 01:10:44.148
That's what I do.

01:10:44.148 --> 01:10:45.690
And they go, well,
then what happens?

01:10:45.690 --> 01:10:47.460
And you go, well,
each step is basically

01:10:47.460 --> 01:10:48.690
minimizing a quadratic
function that's

01:10:48.690 --> 01:10:50.815
under equality constraint,
which is linear algebra.

01:10:50.815 --> 01:10:52.110
Everybody following this?

01:10:52.110 --> 01:10:54.690
OK, so-- all right.

01:10:54.690 --> 01:10:57.930
Next in the hierarchy is we're
going to handle inequality

01:10:57.930 --> 01:10:59.870
constraints.

01:10:59.870 --> 01:11:01.140
So that's the next one.

01:11:01.140 --> 01:11:04.010
It's not as straightforward
as equality constraints,

01:11:04.010 --> 01:11:06.830
but we're going to add
inequality constraints

01:11:06.830 --> 01:11:09.770
and show how to
reduce them to solving

01:11:09.770 --> 01:11:13.940
a sequence of smooth
equality constraint

01:11:13.940 --> 01:11:17.190
minimization problems.

01:11:17.190 --> 01:11:19.620
That's the overall program.

01:11:19.620 --> 01:11:20.120
OK.

01:11:22.790 --> 01:11:24.740
So first, let's talk
about what we're

01:11:24.740 --> 01:11:27.060
going to be looking at here.

01:11:27.060 --> 01:11:28.790
So we're going to
minimize-- we're

01:11:28.790 --> 01:11:31.190
going to solve a problem
where all of the functions

01:11:31.190 --> 01:11:33.170
here are smooth.

01:11:33.170 --> 01:11:36.380
Now, by the way, that's
already done for you

01:11:36.380 --> 01:11:39.320
by something like CVX pi
or something like that

01:11:39.320 --> 01:11:42.440
because it's compiled to a
problem that looks like that.

01:11:42.440 --> 01:11:47.510
So for example, if I said,
minimize the L infinity

01:11:47.510 --> 01:11:50.330
norm of Ax minus b or
something like that,

01:11:50.330 --> 01:11:52.760
that does not satisfy--

01:11:52.760 --> 01:11:54.380
that doesn't work here.

01:11:54.380 --> 01:11:56.690
Oh, subject to x non-negative.

01:11:56.690 --> 01:11:59.240
The x non-negative works,
the objective does not.

01:11:59.240 --> 01:12:01.820
So you would transform
that to an LP.

01:12:01.820 --> 01:12:04.280
And an LP satisfies
this, for sure,

01:12:04.280 --> 01:12:07.250
because all the fi's,
they're all affine.

01:12:07.250 --> 01:12:10.940
And affine is, well,
really very smooth.

01:12:10.940 --> 01:12:12.320
So OK.

01:12:12.320 --> 01:12:14.900
So this is the idea.

01:12:14.900 --> 01:12:18.210
Now, we'll assume that the
problem is strictly feasible.

01:12:18.210 --> 01:12:20.870
And so that's fine.

01:12:20.870 --> 01:12:23.160
And that means that
strong duality holds,

01:12:23.160 --> 01:12:25.710
and there's a dual optimum
and all that kind of stuff.

01:12:25.710 --> 01:12:26.310
OK.

01:12:26.310 --> 01:12:31.290
So examples would be things
like LP, QP, QCQP, GP is one.

01:12:31.290 --> 01:12:36.510
Because GP, all the fi's are
log sum exp of an affine thing.

01:12:36.510 --> 01:12:39.120
That's what a GP is.

01:12:39.120 --> 01:12:41.700
OK, here's one, is
entropy maximization with

01:12:41.700 --> 01:12:43.180
linear inequality constraints.

01:12:43.180 --> 01:12:46.680
So I mean, here
would be a fun one.

01:12:46.680 --> 01:12:48.990
x represents a
probability distribution

01:12:48.990 --> 01:12:50.860
on some finite number of things.

01:12:50.860 --> 01:12:53.468
This basically--
this says minimize--

01:12:53.468 --> 01:12:54.510
this is negative entropy.

01:12:54.510 --> 01:12:59.460
This says, maximize the entropy
over all distributions that

01:12:59.460 --> 01:13:01.373
satisfy linear
equality constraints

01:13:01.373 --> 01:13:02.790
and linear inequality
constraints.

01:13:02.790 --> 01:13:06.720
Now, a linear constraint
on a probability vector

01:13:06.720 --> 01:13:10.470
is actually precisely the same
as just an expectation of any

01:13:10.470 --> 01:13:13.290
of an event, like period.

01:13:13.290 --> 01:13:17.610
It's the expectation of--
sorry, a function on the event

01:13:17.610 --> 01:13:19.908
space, which is these n things.

01:13:19.908 --> 01:13:21.450
So this would be
something like this.

01:13:21.450 --> 01:13:27.710
You say, well, I have a
discrete random variable,

01:13:27.710 --> 01:13:29.990
and I have a whole bunch of--

01:13:29.990 --> 01:13:31.490
I can tell you about
the probability

01:13:31.490 --> 01:13:34.550
that it's in this set,
probability in that set.

01:13:34.550 --> 01:13:36.380
I can give you ranges on those.

01:13:36.380 --> 01:13:39.170
The probability that it's
between here and here

01:13:39.170 --> 01:13:44.390
is-- it's between 0.1 and 0.2,
or something like that right.

01:13:44.390 --> 01:13:47.810
Anyway, then this says,
find me the maximum entropy

01:13:47.810 --> 01:13:50.930
distribution that satisfies
those constraints.

01:13:50.930 --> 01:13:53.750
So OK.

01:13:53.750 --> 01:13:55.920
All right.

01:13:55.920 --> 01:13:56.420
OK.

01:13:56.420 --> 01:13:59.190
So I think I already
mentioned this sort of stuff.

01:13:59.190 --> 01:14:01.640
We'll get to things
like SDPs and SOCPs.

01:14:01.640 --> 01:14:03.730
They're actually better
handled as problems

01:14:03.730 --> 01:14:04.980
with generalized inequalities.

01:14:04.980 --> 01:14:08.240
So we'll handle that later.

01:14:08.240 --> 01:14:09.050
OK.

01:14:09.050 --> 01:14:12.410
So everything is going to
hinge around this thing called

01:14:12.410 --> 01:14:13.340
the log barrier.

01:14:13.340 --> 01:14:15.710
You've seen it before.

01:14:15.710 --> 01:14:20.630
And we'll talk more
about this on Thursday.

01:14:20.630 --> 01:14:24.500
But the story is actually
kind of interesting.

01:14:24.500 --> 01:14:27.920
And it goes like this, right?

01:14:27.920 --> 01:14:29.780
You say, I have
these constraints

01:14:29.780 --> 01:14:33.260
and I don't know how
to handle constraints.

01:14:33.260 --> 01:14:35.690
And a constraint
looks like this.

01:14:35.690 --> 01:14:39.290
If this is fi of x, here's 0.

01:14:39.290 --> 01:14:42.260
And basically, there's a
function that looks like this.

01:14:42.260 --> 01:14:46.790
And this says, if you're
less than or equal to 0,

01:14:46.790 --> 01:14:48.560
I will assess you a charge of 0.

01:14:48.560 --> 01:14:50.690
If you're positive,
I will assess

01:14:50.690 --> 01:14:52.790
a charge of plus infinity.

01:14:52.790 --> 01:14:54.830
So this is the semantics
of a constraint.

01:14:54.830 --> 01:14:59.000
This is actually why people
call it a hard constraint.

01:14:59.000 --> 01:15:02.750
What we're going to
do here is actually--

01:15:02.750 --> 01:15:05.150
it's this exact
same story as dual--

01:15:05.150 --> 01:15:07.052
Lagrangian dual--
Lagrangian duality, I

01:15:07.052 --> 01:15:09.510
don't know if you remember,
but the story started this way.

01:15:09.510 --> 01:15:13.680
And then someone said, I'm going
to approximate this function.

01:15:13.680 --> 01:15:15.390
And you go, yeah,
go ahead, please.

01:15:15.390 --> 01:15:19.420
And they say, yeah,
here's my approximation.

01:15:19.420 --> 01:15:23.110
I mean, this is the beginning
of the story of Lagrangian

01:15:23.110 --> 01:15:27.490
duality, to which a
normal person would say,

01:15:27.490 --> 01:15:32.350
this is not a particularly
good approximation of this.

01:15:32.350 --> 01:15:34.420
I mean, no one could
say that, right?

01:15:34.420 --> 01:15:36.190
Now, that story had
a happy ending right

01:15:36.190 --> 01:15:38.380
because it went on and
on, and it turned out

01:15:38.380 --> 01:15:40.510
for convex things,
it turns out if you

01:15:40.510 --> 01:15:43.900
get these slopes
just right, then

01:15:43.900 --> 01:15:45.400
it actually is a
good approximation.

01:15:45.400 --> 01:15:47.600
Actually, it's an infinitely
good approximation.

01:15:47.600 --> 01:15:49.767
Everybody-- and then there
was a lot of other stuff,

01:15:49.767 --> 01:15:52.017
like these were the prices
and all that kind of stuff.

01:15:52.017 --> 01:15:52.660
That's fine.

01:15:52.660 --> 01:15:53.440
OK.

01:15:53.440 --> 01:15:58.090
This story, actually,
is way better.

01:15:58.090 --> 01:16:00.217
What you're going to--
this story starts this way.

01:16:00.217 --> 01:16:02.050
And we'll continue the
discussion on Friday.

01:16:02.050 --> 01:16:03.175
This story starts this way.

01:16:03.175 --> 01:16:06.580
Someone says, well, I'd like
to apply Newton's method,

01:16:06.580 --> 01:16:08.800
but I have this
function that's 0

01:16:08.800 --> 01:16:10.520
and then goes up
to plus infinity.

01:16:10.520 --> 01:16:13.790
So it's not continuous, it's
not differentiable, it's not--

01:16:13.790 --> 01:16:15.640
and so there's a lot
of things you could do.

01:16:15.640 --> 01:16:18.430
One thing you can actually do
is just apply Newton's method

01:16:18.430 --> 01:16:19.063
to this.

01:16:19.063 --> 01:16:20.105
How well would that work?

01:16:23.701 --> 01:16:24.650
Not well.

01:16:24.650 --> 01:16:26.170
Not well, right?

01:16:26.170 --> 01:16:28.030
In fact, it wouldn't
work at all, right?

01:16:28.030 --> 01:16:30.322
And someone would say, well,
it didn't work because you

01:16:30.322 --> 01:16:32.590
need a smooth function.

01:16:32.590 --> 01:16:36.070
So you'd say, fine, I'll
just make a smooth function.

01:16:36.070 --> 01:16:41.780
I'll make a function that
looks like this, roughly.

01:16:41.780 --> 01:16:43.040
It's a barrier function.

01:16:43.040 --> 01:16:44.990
That's a thing, right?

01:16:44.990 --> 01:16:48.630
It's smooth, it goes
to infinity, and so on.

01:16:48.630 --> 01:16:51.080
I can apply Newton's
method to that.

01:16:51.080 --> 01:16:54.540
And so that's actually how
we're going to start here.

01:16:54.540 --> 01:16:55.220
So we'll quit.

01:16:55.220 --> 01:16:58.250
I think we'll quit for today,
and then we'll continue this.

01:16:58.250 --> 01:17:02.770
Good luck on implementing
your Newton solver.

